\chapter{Shared commands and first steps}
The \texttt{dfg-crc} class is a starting point for a Collaborative Research
Centre proposal and the accompanying CV section.  Authors write the proposal
chapters and maintain structured records for people, institutions, projects,
work packages, and funding.  The class uses those records to create project
headings, references, tables, collaboration plots, Gantt charts, and totals
that stay consistent across the document.

The supplied files include a complete example proposal, example CV, and
customization file.  Start with the generated examples to see how the source
files fit together, then replace their content and metadata with those of your
own centre.  This chapter introduces the commands reused throughout the proposal.
Chapter~\ref{chap:proposal-walkthrough} then follows the proposal itself;
Chapter~\ref{chap:manual-reference} collects the complete key and settings reference.  Applicants should still compare the result
with the current DFG forms and programme rules.

\section{Compiling and generated files}

The maintained source of this bundle is
\texttt{crc-proposal-documentation-and-code.dtx}.  Files such as
\texttt{local/dfg-crc.cls}, \texttt{README.md}, \texttt{CHANGELOG.md}, and
the example files in \texttt{local/} are generated from that source.  For
template or documentation changes, edit the |.dtx| file and regenerate the
derived files.

For a first local compile, use LuaLaTeX through \texttt{latexmk}:
\begin{dispListing*}{title={First local compile}}
make files
cd local
latexmk -lualatex crc-proposal.tex
latexmk -lualatex crc-cv.tex
\end{dispListing*}

The class expects LuaLaTeX.  Calling \texttt{LaTeX} with a capital \texttt{L}
asks TeX for a format file named \texttt{LaTeX.fmt}; use \texttt{lualatex} or
\texttt{latexmk -lualatex} instead.

If a change has no visible effect, check which generated class file is being
loaded.  Regenerate the class and local examples with \texttt{make files}, then
compile the relevant example again.

To update a separately controlled proposal worktree, use
\texttt{make update-class TARGET\_DIR=/path/to/proposal-worktree} for the class
alone.  Use \texttt{make update-all TARGET\_DIR=/path/to/proposal-worktree} to
rebuild the manual and copy \texttt{dfg-crc.cls},
\texttt{crc-proposal-documentation-and-code.pdf}, \texttt{dfg-crc-proposal-example.pdf}, and
\texttt{dfg-crc-cv-example.pdf}.  Both targets require the
destination to be the root of another Git worktree and report the affected
files without committing or pushing them.  They leave the destination's
README, changelog, and licence unchanged.

\section{How to read the examples}
The examples use the same class commands as a proposal. In the template data,
\texttt{per1} is Kim Sample, \texttt{per2} is Alex Example, and \texttt{A01}
is their shared project. The manual has twelve PIs and fifteen active projects:
|A01|--|A06|, |B01|--|B06|, and |Q01|--|Q03|. Research projects include both
continuing and new projects; Q projects demonstrate service, research training,
and administration. |A00| is a former, reference-only project and is excluded
from active counts. Outputs are compiled from the displayed source.

Small examples place code beside its result; wide examples put the result below
and allow it to continue onto another page. Headings inside live examples are
unnumbered so they do not change the manual's outline. The complete shared metadata are
listed once in Section~\ref{sec:manual-fixtures}. Finance examples show their
additional input records at the point of use. Optional settings normally go
immediately after the command, before its required arguments.

\section{Declare records with \texorpdfstring{\cs{addEntry}}{addEntry}}
\begin{docCommand}{addEntry}{\oarg{key-set}\marg{database}\marg{key=value}}
  Adds an entry to the database \meta{database} with the defined
  values from \meta{key=value}.  An |id| is unique within its database.  If a
  later call reuses the same |id|, the class issues a warning, removes all
  earlier rows with that |id|, and stores the later entry instead.  The same
  |id| may still be used in a different database.  Entries without an |id|,
  such as ordinary funding and staff rows, remain append-only.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-declare-a-record-and-retrieve-its-fields}, \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-requested-funding-for-a-project}.
\end{docCommand}

A keyset defines the accepted fields; a database stores the resulting rows.
Omit the optional keyset when it has the same name as the database, for
example |\addEntry{person}{...}|. Give it explicitly for a separate collection,
as below. Braces protect commas inside values. Use stable IDs rather than
repeating names in the prose.

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-declare-a-record-and-retrieve-its-fields}]{Declare a record and retrieve its fields}
\addEntry[person]{ManualContacts}{
  id        = {example-contact},
  firstname = {Kim},
  lastname  = {Sample}
}
\getEntry[ManualContacts]{example-contact}{firstname}\space
\getEntry[ManualContacts]{example-contact}{lastname}
\end{dfgExample}

Store proposal records in |metadata/dfg-metadata.tex|, or in another metadata
file loaded by the proposal. Tables share those records. The standard databases
are |meta| (the CRC), |person|, |project|, |institution|, and |affiliation|;
funding and staff tables use the named collections introduced with each table.
Full field definitions are in Section~\ref{doc:metadata}.

\section{Read, store, and compare fields}
You can get any kind of information back from any database using \refCom{getEntry}:
\begin{docCommand}{getEntry}{\oarg{database}\marg{value of
      id}\marg{column}\oarg{alternative id-field}}
  Retrieve and typeset the value of \meta{column} from the row whose
  id-cell equals \meta{value of id}.

  The optional \meta{database} argument selects the \refPkg{datatool}
  database to query; it defaults to \cs{tmpLocalDB}, which is set by
  the surrounding context (typically by the table-generating command).
  The optional \meta{alternative id-field} argument names the column
  that holds the row identifier and defaults to \texttt{id}; supply a
  different name when the row is keyed on something other than the
  standard id column (for example \texttt{ref-project-id} or
  \texttt{number}).

  The retrieved value is wrapped in \cs{dfgShowTag} before being
  typeset, so when the \refKey{showtags} class option is active the
  output is annotated with a small overlay marker showing the
  originating database, id, and column.  When the option is inactive,
  the value is typeset plain.

  The command performs several checks and emits a warning to the log
  when something is wrong: a \texttt{database-name-not-given} warning
  if \meta{database} is empty, a \texttt{database-not-found} warning
  if no database with the given name exists, and a \texttt{missing-key}
  warning if the row exists but the requested cell is empty or
  undefined.  The last warning is suppressed during \refPkg{tabularray}'s
  measuring phase to avoid duplicate log entries.

  When the \texttt{fastcompile-project} class option is active, the lookup is
  skipped entirely.  Project identifiers are typeset in bold black; other
  row identifiers use a quiet grey placeholder.  Error diagnostics retain
  the configured \texttt{error} colour.  Starred multi-field lookups initialise their destination
  macros to empty values without touching a database.

  \medskip
  Examples:
  \begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-field-lookup-and-alternative-identifier}]{Field lookup and alternative identifier}
\getEntry[person]{per9}{lastname}\par
\getEntry[project]{A06}{name}[number]\par
\def\tmpLocalDB{person}
\getEntry{per9}{firstname}
\end{dfgExample}


  When several columns from the same row are needed, prefer the starred
  form \refCom{getEntry*}, which performs only a single row lookup.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-field-lookup-and-alternative-identifier}.
\end{docCommand}

\begin{docCommand}{getEntry*}{\oarg{database}\marg{value of
      id}\marg{assignments}\oarg{alternative id-field}}
  Starred variant of \cs{getEntry}.  Retrieves multiple columns from the
  same row in a single lookup and assigns each value to the macro named
  on the left-hand side of the assignment.

  The \meta{assignments} argument is a comma-separated list of
  \meta{\textbackslash macro}\,\texttt{=}\,\meta{column} pairs.  For each
  pair, the value of \meta{column} for the row whose id-cell equals
  \meta{value of id} is stored in \meta{\textbackslash macro}.  Unlike
  the unstarred form, this variant does not print anything and does not
  wrap results in \cs{dfgShowTag} -- the caller is responsible for
  formatting the retrieved values.

  Missing fields trigger the same \texttt{missing-key} warning as the
  unstarred form (suppressed during \pkg{tabularray}'s measuring phase).

  \medskip
  Example:
  \begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-retrieve-several-fields-at-once}]{Retrieve several fields at once}
\getEntry*[person]{per8}{
  \exampleFirst = firstname,
  \exampleLast = lastname,
  \exampleDegree = academicdegree
}
\exampleDegree\space
\exampleFirst\space\exampleLast
\end{dfgExample}
  This is preferable over multiple \cs{getEntry} calls when several
  fields from the same row are needed: the row is located once instead
  of once per field.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-retrieve-several-fields-at-once}.
\end{docCommand}

\begin{docCommand}{tmpLocalDB}{\marg{database}}
  Define the database locally (e.g. within a group) so you don't need to repeat it
  as \oarg{database} for e.g. \refCom{getEntry}.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-field-lookup-and-alternative-identifier}.
\end{docCommand}


\begin{docCommand}{compareEntry}{\oarg{database}\marg{id}\marg{field}\marg{value}
    \marg{true}\marg{false}}
  Compare an entry with the \meta{id} of \meta{database} against \meta{value}
  and show either \meta{true} or \meta{false}.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-compare-a-database-entry-against-something}, \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-conditionally-print-an-affiliation}.
\end{docCommand}

For example, compare the surname of |per6| against an expected value.

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-compare-a-database-entry-against-something}]{Compare a database entry against something.}
\compareEntry[person]{per6}{lastname}{Hartmann}
  {The surname matches.}{The surname differs.}
\end{dfgExample}

The next example prints an affiliation only when the institution refers to it.
\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-conditionally-print-an-affiliation}]{Conditionally print an affiliation}
\compareEntry[institution]{ins3}{ref-affiliation-id}{aff3}
  {\getEntry[affiliation]{aff3}{name}}
  {Another university}
\end{dfgExample}

\section{Follow references between records}
A |ref-institution-id| value links a person to institutions; each institution's
|ref-affiliation-id| links it to a university or other parent organisation.
Use one brace argument per step, ending with the field to print. Multiple IDs
are followed automatically, so prose stays correct when affiliations change.

\begin{docCommand}{get}{\oarg{database}\marg{id}\marg{field or reference}\ldots}
  Read a field directly, or follow metadata references before reading the
  final field. Each intermediate argument names a destination database and
  follows the current row's |ref-<database>-id| field. The source database
  defaults to \cs{tmpLocalDB}. No institution or affiliation ID needs to be
  repeated in the document text.

  \begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-follow-metadata-references}]{Follow metadata references}
\get[person]{per7}{lastname}\par
\get[person]{per7}{institution}{name}\par
\get[person]{per7}{institution}{affiliation}{name}\par
\get[project]{B04}{person}{lastname}\par
\get[RequestedFundingStaff]{per6}{person}[ref-person-id]{institution}{name}
\end{dfgExample}

  The full reference-key spelling also works:
  |\get[person]{MM}{ref-institution-id}{ref-affiliation-id}{name}|.
  Traversal is generic: a |ref-other-unit-id| field points to rows of the
  |other-unit| database. The source may be any database populated by
  \refCom{addEntry}, including one using a separately named keyset. Each
  destination is matched by its |id| field. The lookup follows only the
  explicitly requested path; an affiliation reached through an institution
  therefore needs both steps.

  A reference value may contain multiple comma-separated IDs. All branches
  are followed in stored order, and the resulting values are separated by
  commas. Repeated destination rows are printed once, in first-occurrence
  order. For example, two institutions of one university yield two institution
  names but only one affiliation name when the path ends at that university.
  Different rows with the same name are still distinct results.

  Missing databases, rows, fields, and empty reference lists use the existing
  lookup warnings and contribute no value; valid branches still render without
  dangling separators. The final value retains its LaTeX formatting and the
  \refKey{showtags} annotation identifies the destination row and field.
  Focused fast compilation consumes the path but prints the source ID
  placeholder, just like \refCom{getEntry}.

  With no following step, a full reference key returns its raw ID list:
  |\get[person]{MM}{ref-institution-id}|. An existing scalar field takes
  precedence over a shorthand relation name, so a following prose group is
  not absorbed. Use the explicit |ref-<database>-id| spelling if the names
  conflict. For a custom source identifier column, append the existing
  |[id-field]| argument after the first field/reference argument; destination
  rows still use |id|. The starred |\get*| form delegates to
  \refCom{getEntry*} for assignments and does not traverse references.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-follow-metadata-references}, \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-raw-reference-ids-and-resolved-organisation-names}.
\end{docCommand}

Two institution IDs may lead to the same affiliation. The final destination
is printed once, so this also works when a person's metadata changes later.
\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-raw-reference-ids-and-resolved-organisation-names}]{Raw reference IDs and resolved organisation names}
IDs: \get[person]{per7}{ref-institution-id}\par
Institutions: \get[person]{per7}{institution}{name}\par
Affiliation:
\get[person]{per7}{institution}{affiliation}{name}
\end{dfgExample}

A lookup may branch at several steps: from a project to its PIs, then to their
institutions and affiliations. Shared affiliations are still deduplicated.
\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-find-the-affiliations-behind-a-project}]{Find the affiliations behind a project}
\getProject*{B04}\par
PIs: \get[project]{B04}{person}{lastname}\par
Affiliations:
\get[project]{B04}{person}{institution}{affiliation}{name}
\end{dfgExample}

\section{Names and references in prose}
To quickly get a name of a person there is a dedicated command:
\begin{docCommand}{getName}{\marg{<id>}}
  Get last name of person that has the \meta{id}.
  This sets the database to \texttt{person}.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-names-with-and-without-academic-titles}.
\end{docCommand}

\begin{docCommands}[
    doc name = getFullName
  ]{
    {doc parameter = \sarg\marg{id}},
    {doc parameter = \marg{id}}
  }
  Get the full name of the person with the \meta{id}.
  The starred version adds the academic title in front.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-names-with-and-without-academic-titles}.
\end{docCommands}

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-names-with-and-without-academic-titles}]{Names with and without academic titles}
\getName{per9}\par
\getFullName{per9}\par
\getFullName*{per9}
\end{dfgExample}

PI and project commands also have all-lowercase aliases, with identical
arguments, starred forms, formatting, and links. For example,
|\getpiwithproject{per6}| is equivalent to |\getPIWithProject{per6}|, and
|\getpiofproject*{B05}| is equivalent to |\getPIofProject*{B05}|.
This also applies to |\getPI|, |\getPIsWithRole|, |\getProject|,
|\getProjectColor|, |\getProjectCount|, |\getProjectWithPI|,
|\getPIOfProjectWithProject|, and the legacy commands |\getPIProject| and
|\getProjectPI|. Legacy aliases share the same once-per-command, per-LaTeX-run deprecation
warning as their canonical spelling.
The mixed spellings |\getPIwithProject|, |\getProjectwithPI|, and
|\getPIOfProject| are accepted as well. Other capitalizations remain
case-sensitive in TeX.

\begin{docCommands}[
    doc name = getPI
  ]{
    {doc parameter = \sarg\marg{id-list}},
    {doc parameter = \marg{id-list}},
    {doc parameter = \marg{id-list}\oarg{text}}
  }
  Reference one or multiple PIs from the \texttt{person} database.  The
  \meta{id-list} is a comma-separated list of person ids.  By default the
  command prints the lastname; the starred version also prints the firstname.
  If multiple PI records share a lastname, unstarred references automatically
  add the first given-name initial. For example, PI records for Kai Markus
  Schneider and Carolin V. Schneider are printed as \textbf{K.~Schneider} and
  \textbf{C.~Schneider}. If the initial is also shared, the full firstname is
  used instead. The comparison includes all records with role \texttt{PI} or
  \texttt{Co-PI}, independently of funding period or project selection.
  Project-first references inherit this distinction, for example
  \textbf{A04 (K.~Schneider)} and \textbf{B09 (C.~Schneider)}.
  With the optional \meta{text} argument, \meta{text} is printed instead, but
  the hyperlink still points to the PI.
  Set \texttt{PIReferenceFirstName=true} with \refCom{changeSettings} to make
  the unstarred command include the firstname too. The starred form always
  includes it. This setting also applies to \refCom{getPIProject} and
  \refCom{getPIofProject}. \refCom{getProjectPI} keeps project PI names to
  lastnames with automatic disambiguation unless its starred form is used.
  Shared PI surnames use the initial rule even with
  \texttt{PIReferenceFirstName=true}; use the star to force full firstnames.
  Explicit display text bypasses automatic disambiguation. Fast project
  compilation retains the usual ID placeholder without reading the database.

  The printed name is typeset bold, coloured with \texttt{referencePI}, and
  linked to the corresponding person's row in
  \cs{printTable}|{ProjectLeaders}|.  Each row of that table provides the
  destination \texttt{pi:}\meta{id}.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-reference-one-or-multiple-pis}.
\end{docCommands}

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-reference-one-or-multiple-pis}]{Reference one or multiple PIs.}
\getPI{per6}\par
\getPI*{per6,per8}\par
\getPI{spokesperson}[the spokesperson]
\end{dfgExample}

\begin{docCommand}{getProject}{\sarg\marg{id-list}\oarg{text}}
  Reference one or multiple projects from the \texttt{project} database.  The
  \meta{id-list} is a comma-separated list of project ids.  By default the
  command prints the project number; with the optional \meta{text} argument,
  \meta{text} is printed instead, but the hyperlink still points to the
  project.  In CV mode the command prints the same text without a hyperlink.
  The starred form always uses this compact project-only representation,
  independently of \refKey{projectPIOncePerSection} and its first-reference
  state.
  Projects declared with \refKey{reference-only}|=true| are never hyperlinked;
  instead, \enquote{former project} is appended to make the missing project
  section explicit.

  The printed project reference is typeset bold and coloured according to the
  first letter of the project id. Multiple project references are joined as a
  natural-language list with commas and a final \enquote{and}. With
  \refKey{projectPIOncePerSection}|=true|, the command behaves like the
  unstarred \refCom{getProjectPI}: the first reference to each project in a
  section includes its PIs and later references use the compact project form.
  Generated tables and lists remain compact.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-reference-one-or-multiple-projects}.
\end{docCommand}

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-reference-one-or-multiple-projects}]{Reference one or multiple projects.}
\changeSettings{projectPIOncePerSection=false}
\getProject{A03,B04,Q01}\par
\getProject{A06}[the modelling project]\par
\getProject*{A00}
\end{dfgExample}

\begin{docCommand}{getProjectCount}{\oarg{area}}
  Print the number of active projects.  A project is active when its
  \refKey{project-status} is \texttt{C} or \texttt{N} and
  \refKey{consider-for-lists} is \texttt{true} and
  \refKey{reference-only} is \texttt{false}.  With no optional argument all
  active projects are counted.  Any explicit \refKey{project-area} value may
  be supplied to restrict the result; where that metadata is absent, the first
  character of the project ID is used.  All area totals are computed together
  on first use and cached for subsequent calls.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-count-active-projects}.
\end{docCommand}

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-count-active-projects}]{Count active projects.}
All: \getProjectCount\par
Area A: \getProjectCount[A]\par
Area B: \getProjectCount[B]\par
Area Q: \getProjectCount[Q]
\end{dfgExample}

\begin{docCommands}[
    doc name = CRC
  ]{
    {doc parameter = \marg{crc-number}},
    {doc parameter = \sarg\marg{crc-number}},
    {doc parameter = \sarg\oarg{crc-title}\marg{crc-number}}
  }
  Print a CRC reference.  For the current CRC, identified by the
  \refKey{number} field of the \texttt{meta} entry with id \texttt{crc},
  the unstarred command prints the short form stored in \cs{thisCRC}; the
  starred command prints the long form with the CRC title.

  For a different CRC number, the unstarred command prints only the short
  reference, while the starred form prints a long reference.  If
  \meta{crc-title} is given, it is used as the title of the external CRC;
  otherwise the command falls back to the CRC number.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-reference-current-and-external-crcs}.
\end{docCommands}

\begin{docCommands}[
    doc name = TRR
  ]{
    {doc parameter = \marg{trr-number}},
    {doc parameter = \sarg\marg{trr-number}},
    {doc parameter = \sarg\oarg{trr-title}\marg{trr-number}}
  }
  Print a CRC/TRR reference.  The command follows the same rules as
  \refCom{CRC}, but uses \enquote{CRC/TRR} as the printed programme
  type.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-reference-current-and-external-crcs}.
\end{docCommands}

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-reference-current-and-external-crcs}]{Reference current and external CRCs.}
\CRC{9999}\par
\CRC*{9999}\par
\CRC*[External CRC title]{999}\par
\TRR*[External CRC/TRR title]{999}
\end{dfgExample}

\begin{docCommands}[
    doc name = getPIProject
  ]{
    {doc parameter = \sarg\marg{person-id-list}},
    {doc parameter = \marg{person-id-list}}
  }
  Print one or multiple PIs together with their assigned projects.  The
  \meta{person-id-list} is a comma-separated list of ids from the
  \texttt{person} database.  For each PI, the command reads
  \refKey{ref-project-id} from the person entry and prints the referenced
  project identifiers in parentheses using \cs{getProject}|*|, without
  repeating the projects' PI names.

  The starred version prints firstnames in addition to lastnames.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-print-pis-with-their-projects}.
\end{docCommands}

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-print-pis-with-their-projects}]{Print PIs with their projects.}
\changeSettings{projectPIOncePerSection=false}
\getPIProject{per6,per11}\par\medskip
\getPIProject*{per6,per11}
\end{dfgExample}

Use \cs{getPIWithProject} for the same person-first output. It takes person
ids and prints each PI followed by their project list. The legacy
\cs{getPIProject} remains available but warns that it is deprecated.

\begin{docCommands}[
    doc name = getProjectPI
  ]{
    {doc parameter = \sarg\marg{project-id-list}},
    {doc parameter = \marg{project-id-list}}
  }
  Print one or multiple projects together with their assigned PIs.  The
  \meta{project-id-list} is a comma-separated list of ids from the
  \texttt{project} database.  For each project, the command reads
  \refKey{ref-person-id} from the project entry and prints the referenced PIs
  in parentheses using \refCom{getPI}.

  If a project id is identical to the current project established by
  \refCom{printProjectHead}, only the project itself is printed, just as with
  \refCom{getProject}; its PIs are omitted.  In a list containing several
  projects, this exception applies only to the matching project.

  By default, PI names are shown only for the first \refCom{getProject} or
  \refCom{getProjectPI} reference to each project within a section. Both
  commands share the same remembered state, and a new section resets it.
  Generated tables and lists remain compact. Set
  \refKey{projectPIOncePerSection}|=false| with \refCom{changeSettings} to make
  \refCom{getProject} always compact and \refCom{getProjectPI} always expanded.

  The starred version prints firstnames in addition to lastnames.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-print-projects-with-their-pis}.
\end{docCommands}

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-print-projects-with-their-pis}]{Print projects with their PIs.}
\changeSettings{projectPIOncePerSection=false}
\getProjectPI{A03,B04}\par\medskip
\getProjectPI*{A03,B04}
\end{dfgExample}

Use \cs{getProjectWithPI} for the same project-first output. It takes project
ids and prints each project followed by its PI list. The legacy
\cs{getProjectPI} remains available but warns that it is deprecated.

\begin{docCommand}{getPIOfProjectWithProject}{\sarg\marg{project-id-list}}
  Print the PIs of one or more projects, followed by each project's compact
  reference in parentheses. The argument is a comma-separated list of project
  ids, matching the project named after \enquote{OfProject} in the command.
  The starred form includes firstnames; the
  \texttt{PIReferenceFirstName=true} setting also enables firstnames in the
  unstarred form.
\end{docCommand}

\begin{docCommand}{getPIofProject}{\sarg\oarg{properties}\marg{project-id}\oarg{delimiter}}
  Print the PIs assigned to a project.  The command reads
  \refKey{ref-person-id} from the project entry and then prints selected
  person fields from the \texttt{person} database. Each name links to its row
  in the PI table and uses the same reference styling as \refCom{getPI}. The optional
  \meta{properties} argument is a comma-separated list of fields to print and
  defaults to \texttt{lastname}.  Typical values are
  \texttt{academicdegree}, \texttt{firstname}, and \texttt{lastname}.  The
  optional \meta{delimiter} separates multiple PIs and defaults to |\,/\,|.

  The starred form defaults to |firstname,lastname| and joins names as
  ``Alice Example, Bob Sample and Carol Demo''. With two PIs it uses only
  ``and''; with one PI it adds no separator. Explicit properties still override
  the default. In the starred form, an explicit trailing delimiter replaces
  only the final ``and'' separator; earlier names remain comma-separated.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-print-the-pis-of-a-project}.
\end{docCommand}

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-print-the-pis-of-a-project}]{Print the PIs of a project.}
\getPIofProject{B05}\par
\getPIofProject[firstname,lastname]{B05}[; ]\par
\getPIofProject*{Q02}\par
\getPIofProject*{Q03}\par
\getPIofProject*[lastname]{Q03}
\end{dfgExample}

\section{Change shared settings}
\begin{docCommand}{changeSettings}{\marg{key=value}}
  Customize fonts, logo paths, colors, \refKey{thumbindex}, and other class
  defaults.  The former \cs{changeSetting} spelling remains available as a
  compatibility alias.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-set-proposal-text-in-the-customization-file}.
\end{docCommand}
Put proposal-wide settings in |preamble/dfg-customization.tex|. For example,
|\changeSettings{salary@PhD=83700}| sets the annual salary used in funding
calculations. Table, list, and plot options apply to a single call; shared
settings establish defaults. The full list is in Section~\ref{doc:customization}.

\begin{docCommand}{getValue}{\marg{customization key}}
  Read the current value of any key accepted by \refCom{changeSettings}.
  The command is expandable and follows local changes, so numeric values can
  also be used in \cs{num} and calculations. For example:
\begin{dispListing*}{title={Using customization values in text}}
Housing costs are \num{\getValue{animalHousing@mouse}}\,EUR per mouse per week.
The annual postdoctoral rate is \num{\getValue{salary@postDoc}}\,EUR.
The configured font is \getValue{font}.
Thumb index enabled: \getValue{thumbindex}.
The reference color is \getValue{color@reference@PI}.
\end{dispListing*}
  These examples read the configured values without changing them. With no
  override, \texttt{animalHousing@mouse} returns \texttt{0.75}; the getter
  uses the same stored rate as the animal-housing table. It returns a value
  without a currency or unit suffix; add those in the surrounding text.

  Booleans return \texttt{true} or \texttt{false}; dimensions return points.
  Automatic thumb-index margins return \texttt{auto}, and orientation returns
  \texttt{inwards} or \texttt{outwards}. Text and formatting values retain their
  LaTeX commands. The compatibility keys \texttt{biblatexstyle} and
  \texttt{biblatexstyleFeature} read the current document mode's CV or proposal
  setting. Leading and trailing spaces around a key are ignored; unknown keys
  raise an error. This interface covers customization keys, not arbitrary
  commands or packages defined in the customization file.
\end{docCommand}

\begin{docKeys}[doc parameter={=\meta{text}}]{
  {doc name=dfgLocation}, {doc name=dfgDate},
  {doc name=dfgTitle}, {doc name=InfoTeX}
}
  Override the matching public commands through \cs{changeSettings}, including
  from |preamble/dfg-customization.tex|. The defaults are |<Location>|,
  |<date>|, the quoted CRC name from metadata, and |LuaLaTeX (via Overleaf).|,
  respectively. The first three commands append \cs{xspace}; |InfoTeX| prints
  its value exactly. Values are stored without expansion, so references and
  formatting can be used in them. No fixed submission date is supplied.
  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-set-proposal-text-in-the-customization-file}.
\end{docKeys}

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-set-proposal-text-in-the-customization-file}]{Set proposal text in the customization file}
\changeSettings{
  dfgLocation = {Example City},
  dfgDate     = {30 September 2026},
  dfgTitle    = {Collaborative research programme},
  InfoTeX    = {LuaLaTeX, local build}
}
\dfgTitle\par
\dfgLocation, \dfgDate\par
\InfoTeX
\end{dfgExample}

These macros already exist when the customization file is read, so direct
overrides with \cs{renewcommand} work there too. For the three
space-aware commands, include |\xspace| yourself when using |\renewcommand|.
Definitions supplied before loading the class are preserved until explicitly
overridden. If both interfaces assign a value, the last assignment wins.

\section{Tables, lists, and plots: one calling convention}
The dispatcher selects a named output and passes its arguments to it. Put
settings in the leading square brackets. A simple target needs one pair of
braces; a target with arguments uses an outer pair around the entire sequence:
|\printTable{ProjectLeaders}|, |\printTable{{Projects}{A}{Area title}}|.
The following chapter introduces each built-in target beside its result.

\begin{docCommand}{printTable}{\oarg{options}\marg{name}}
  Print one of the predefined template tables.  The \meta{name} is the table
  base name without the suffix \texttt{Table}; for example
  \texttt{ProjectLeaders} calls \cs{ProjectLeadersTable}.  If \meta{name} is a
  braced list, the first item is used as the table base name and the remaining
  items are passed as arguments to that table command.  The gap above a table
  is set by |presep=6pt| in the shared \cs{SetTblrOuter} call below; adjust
  that value to change the gap above generated tables.
  Put options before the table specification. For example,
  |\printTable[all-sexes]{GenderEqualityProjectLeaders}| selects the expanded
  gender overview for that call only.
  The command inserts \cs{par} before and after its output, so surrounding
  text always starts in separate paragraphs, even without blank source lines.
  Directly after a heading, it also inserts a zero-height box and a no-break
  penalty. This prevents the table from overlapping the heading at a page
  break; a manual \cs{null} before \cs{printTable} is unnecessary.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-project-leaders-and-their-affiliations}, \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-requested-funding-for-a-project}, \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-laboratory-animals-purchase-housing-and-funding}.
\end{docCommand}

\begin{docCommand}{printList}{\oarg{options}\marg{name}}
  Print one of the predefined template lists.  The \meta{name} is the list base
  name without the suffix \texttt{List}; for example
  \texttt{ParticipatingInstitutions} calls
  \cs{ParticipatingInstitutionsList}.  The optional \meta{options} are passed
  to the generated list command.  Put sorting, formatting, database, and
  raster settings together in this leading option list, as with
  \cs{printPlot}.  Both |{ParticipatingInstitutions}| and
  |{{ParticipatingInstitutions}}| are accepted as the list specification.
  Older trailing options remain supported, with leading settings taking
  precedence.  A person's institutions use
  |\printList{{Institutions}{per1}}|; that form needs the person ID, while
  |\printList{ParticipatingInstitutions}| lists participating institutions
  across the proposal, with affiliation names in bold.


  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-flowing-institution-columns}, \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-alphabetical-institution-raster}, \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-one-person-with-multiple-institutions}.
\end{docCommand}

\cs{printPlot} uses the same convention: |\printPlot[options]{{type}{project-id}}|.
Within a project, omit the ID to use that project. The complete option and
result examples follow the work-package declarations in
Section~\ref{sec:manual-plot-examples}.

\chapter{Build the proposal in order}
\label{chap:proposal-walkthrough}
\label{sec:manual-table-guide}
Follow the generated proposal from the title page through general information,
finances, individual projects, bylaws, declarations, and back matter. Each
output example pairs its source with its result. All sixteen built-in table
targets, all three lists, and both plot types appear at their point of use.
The shared metadata are documented once in Section~\ref{sec:manual-fixtures};
table-specific records are shown directly before their printing command.

\section{Title page and applicant universities}

\begin{docCommand}{dfgApplicantAffiliationsTF}{\marg{singular}\marg{plural}}
  Use \meta{singular} when |meta.ref-affiliation-id| contains one applicant
  affiliation and \meta{plural} when it contains several.  The title page,
  applicant-university section heading, bylaws, and declarations use this
  helper for their grammar.  \cs{theCRCaffiliation} and
  \cs{theCRCaffiliationLocation} contain the complete human-readable name and
  location lists.  The first id is the coordinating university and is exposed
  as \cs{theCRCCoordinatingAffiliation}; any remaining ids are exposed as
  \cs{theCRCOtherApplicantAffiliations}, matching the two separate fields on
  the DFG title page.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-choose-applicant-wording-from-metadata}.
\end{docCommand}

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-choose-applicant-wording-from-metadata}]{Choose applicant wording from metadata}
\dfgApplicantAffiliationsTF
  {Applicant university:}
  {Applicant universities:}
\theCRCaffiliation.
\end{dfgExample}

\begin{docCommand}{crcLogo}{\oarg{graphics-options}}
  Print the CRC logo.  The optional \meta{graphics-options} are passed to
  \cs{includegraphics}.  The actual file is configured with
  \refKey{crcLogoPath} through \cs{changeSettings} and remains available as
  \cs{crcLogoPath}.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-configure-and-print-the-title-page-logos}.
\end{docCommand}

\begin{docCommand}{affiliationLogo}{\oarg{graphics-options}}
  Print the affiliation logo.  The optional \meta{graphics-options} are passed
  to \cs{includegraphics}.  The actual file is configured with
  \refKey{affiliationLogoPath} through \cs{changeSettings} and remains
  available as \cs{affiliationLogoPath}.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-configure-and-print-the-title-page-logos}.
\end{docCommand}

\begin{docCommand}{Hrule}{\oarg{key=value}}
  Print a configurable horizontal rule.  The available keys are
  \texttt{color}, \texttt{above}, \texttt{below}, and \texttt{thickness}.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-a-horizontal-rule-with-custom-spacing}.
\end{docCommand}

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-a-horizontal-rule-with-custom-spacing}]{A horizontal rule with custom spacing}
Scientific objectives
\Hrule[color=blue,thickness=.6pt,above=4pt,below=6pt]
Experimental approach
\end{dfgExample}

Use your own logo files in a proposal. This example substitutes the bundled
placeholder image so the size options can be tried without external files.

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-configure-and-print-the-title-page-logos}]{Configure and print the title-page logos}
\changeSettings{
  crcLogoPath=example-image,
  affiliationLogoPath=example-image
}
\crcLogo[width=.4\linewidth]\quad
\affiliationLogo[width=.4\linewidth]
\end{dfgExample}

\section{General information}
\subsection{Key data: governing bodies and contact details}
\begin{docCommand}{getPersonInformation}{\oarg{database}\marg{person-id-list}}
  Print full PI contact details, including institutions and contact fields.
  The database defaults to |person|. Use explicit person IDs for a selected
  pair; use \cs{getPIsWithRole} below to select people by their metadata role.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-contact-details-for-two-selected-pis}.
\end{docCommand}

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-contact-details-for-two-selected-pis},dfg wide]{Contact details for two selected PIs}
\getPersonInformation[person]{per1,per2}
\end{dfgExample}

\begin{docCommand}{getPIsWithRole}{\oarg{options}\marg{role}}
  Print persons with the given \meta{role} in PI boxes.  Put all options
  before the role, for example |database=person,raster columns=2|.
  The database defaults to \texttt{person}.  Only persons belonging to
  the current funding period are shown.  The default three-column raster
  has a 12\,pt column gap.  Rows of boxes and the content after the raster
  are separated by 20\,pt.  Raster settings in \meta{options} are passed to
  the underlying \refPkg{tcolorbox} raster and may override those defaults.
  Use |academicdegree-below| to place the academic degree on a separate
  line below the name, inside the same PI-box title.  This applies only to
  the current call; omitting the option or setting |academicdegree-below=false|
  keeps the degree inline after the name.  For example:
  |\getPIsWithRole[academicdegree-below]{steering-committee-member}|.
  The contact markers in each PI box are controlled globally with
  |\changeSettings{PIcontactStyle=icons}| (the default),
  |PIcontactStyle=text| for the labels ``Phone:'' and ``E-Mail:'', or
  |PIcontactStyle=none| to suppress both markers.
  Older calls with a bare database name or trailing raster options remain
  supported; leading settings take precedence over trailing settings.

  In both this raster and the two-column raster of \cs{getPersonInformation},
  titles are ragged right and top-aligned.  Each title reserves the height of
  the tallest title in its raster, keeping the contact information aligned
  when names or academic degrees wrap.  Equal-height groups settle over
  successive LaTeX runs; a clean build can require three passes.
  Phone and email each remain unbroken, but their one-em separating space
  permits a line break when both contacts do not fit on the same line.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-print-all-pis-with-a-given-role}.
\end{docCommand}

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-print-all-pis-with-a-given-role},dfg wide]{Print all PIs with a given role.}
\getPIsWithRole[raster columns=2]{steering-committee-member}
\end{dfgExample}

\subsection{Project leaders}
The table resolves each PI's institutions and affiliations from the shared
metadata (Section~\ref{sec:manual-fixtures}). Declare those records once;
changing a name or institution then updates every table and reference.

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-project-leaders-and-their-affiliations},dfg wide,listing above* text]{Project leaders and their affiliations}
\printTable{ProjectLeaders}
\end{dfgExample}

\subsection{Participating institutions}
  Use |columns=2| or |columns=3| to flow affiliation groups down balanced
  columns, keeping each affiliation with its institutions. Short groups follow
  one another without equal-height row padding. Reading order is down each
  column. The gap defaults to 12\,pt; set |column sep=16pt| to change it.
  Flowing lists start a new paragraph with \cs{medskipamount} space above them.

  Without |columns|, participating institutions use one raster cell per
  affiliation, including its institutions. The default is one column,
  with a 12\,pt column gap and
  a 10\,pt row gap.  Pass |raster columns=2| or other \refPkg{tcolorbox}
  raster options through \meta{options}.  Sorting defaults to |order=count|;
  |order=alpha| sorts affiliations alphabetically.  The bare |count| and
  |alpha| options remain accepted.
  \begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-flowing-institution-columns},dfg wide]{Flowing institution columns}
\printList[columns=2]{ParticipatingInstitutions}
\end{dfgExample}

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-three-flowing-institution-columns},dfg wide]{Three flowing institution columns}
\printList[columns=3,column sep=16pt]{ParticipatingInstitutions}
\end{dfgExample}

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-alphabetical-institution-raster},dfg wide]{Alphabetical institution raster}
\printList[order=alpha,raster columns=2]{ParticipatingInstitutions}
\end{dfgExample}

