\paragraph{Person-specific institution lists}
\label{sec:manual-person-lists}
Use |\printList{{Institutions}{per7}}| to print the institutions assigned to
person \texttt{per7}.  The person ID is required.  For a non-default database,
use |\printList[database=other]{{Institutions}{per7}}|; the database defaults to
\texttt{person}.  Put formatting options in the same leading list, as in
|\printList[database=person,abbreviation,narrow]{{Institutions}{per7}}|.
The older bare-database and trailing-formatting forms remain supported.
The list reads
\refKey{ref-institution-id} from the person entry and then resolves the
referenced entries in the \texttt{institution} and \texttt{affiliation}
databases. Options are \texttt{abbreviation}, \texttt{narrow},
\texttt{noaddress}, \texttt{nozip}, and \texttt{nolocation}.

  In the standard layout, every institution prints the institution name,
  affiliation name, street, ZIP code, location, and country when these values
  are available.  If a person references more than one institution, the entries
  are separated by a paragraph break with a small vertical gap.  If an
  institution row has an empty \refKey{name}, the output starts directly with
  the affiliation name.

  \begin{description}
    \item[\texttt{abbreviation}] Use the institution's
      \refKey{abbreviation} instead of its full \refKey{name}.  If the
      abbreviation is absent, the full name is used for compatibility.
    \item[\texttt{narrow}] Print a compact inline form.  Fields inside one
      institution are separated by commas and multiple institutions by
      semicolons.  This is useful in narrow CV/table cells.
    \item[\texttt{noaddress}] Omit the street address.
    \item[\texttt{nozip}] Omit the ZIP code.
    \item[\texttt{nolocation}] Omit the location and country.
  \end{description}
The former \cs{dfgInstitutionList} spelling remains as a compatibility alias.

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-one-person-with-multiple-institutions}]{One person with multiple institutions}
\get[person]{per7}{ref-institution-id}\par
\printList{{Institutions}{per7}}
\medskip
\printList[abbreviation,narrow,noaddress,nozip,nolocation]
  {{Institutions}{per7}}
\end{dfgExample}

\subsection{Frame-text references}
\begin{docCommand}{printFrameBibliography}{\oarg{biblatex options}}
  Print the frame-text bibliography, forwarding the optional arguments to
  \cs{printbibliography} and preserving its normal font size. With
  \refKey{emphasizeEntries} enabled, a complete entry is bold when its
  \texttt{keywords} field contains \texttt{frametext-highlight}. Project
  highlight keywords do not affect this list; the frame keyword does not
  affect project lists or inline citations. Like project bibliographies,
  output is suppressed when \refKey{fastcompile-project-show-publications}
  is disabled.
\end{docCommand}

Add the keyword to the publication's existing keywords, for example
|keywords = {pabst-A,B06-highlight,frametext-highlight}|. The keyword controls
formatting, not inclusion: cite the entry or add it with \cs{nocite} as usual.
Enable \refKey{emphasizeEntries} in \cs{changeSettings}, then use:
\begin{dispListing}
\defbibnote{frame-references}{%
  \usekomafont{subsection}%
  \bfseries\sffamily References
}
\printFrameBibliography[
  prenote={frame-references},
  heading=none
]
\end{dispListing}
Replace the plain \cs{printbibliography} call in existing chapter 1 files;
template updates preserve those edited files.

