\subsubsection{Use the same statistics in prose}
\begin{description}
  \item[\cs{getPositionGenderPhaseAmount}]
    \oarg{database}\marg{position}\marg{gender}\marg{period} prints the count
    for one position/gender/period tuple.
    See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-person-counts-and-percentages-for-a-funding-period}.

  \item[\cs{getPositionPhaseTotal}]
    \oarg{database}\marg{position}\marg{period} prints the all-gender total.
    See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-person-counts-and-percentages-for-a-funding-period}.

  \item[\cs{getGenderPhaseTotal}]
    \oarg{database}\marg{gender}\marg{period} prints the all-position total.
    See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-person-counts-and-percentages-for-a-funding-period}.

  \item[\cs{getPositionGenderPhasePercentage}]
    \oarg{database}\marg{position}\marg{gender}\marg{period} prints the
    rounded integer percentage value within a position.
    See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-person-counts-and-percentages-for-a-funding-period}.

  \item[\cs{getGenderPhasePercentage}]
    \oarg{database}\marg{gender}\marg{period} prints the rounded integer
    percentage value across all counted positions.
    See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-person-counts-and-percentages-for-a-funding-period}.

\end{description}

The period argument is the funding-period number, not a calendar year.
Percentage commands return a number; append |\%| when using it in prose.
The commands below retrieve the numbers printed in section~1.4.2~B for any
funding period, independently of which proposal period is currently selected.
Use |f|, |m|, or |d| for the gender. The supported table positions are
|postDoc|, |groupLeader|, |prof-W2|, and |prof-W3|. The total-row commands
sum these four positions and count each person with |role=PI| once per period;
a person belonging to several periods is counted in each matching period.
The optional database defaults to |person|. Percentages are rounded to the
nearest integer and return zero if their denominator is zero. These are
person statistics; calculating the share of projects with female leadership
requires a separate calculation that counts each project once.

\begin{dispListing}
% First funding period: female W3 professors, number and percentage.
\getPositionGenderPhaseAmount{prof-W3}{f}{1}
\getPositionGenderPhasePercentage{prof-W3}{f}{1}\%
% All genders within the same career category.
\getPositionPhaseTotal{prof-W3}{1}
% First-period total row: female project leaders, number and percentage.
\getGenderPhaseTotal{f}{1}
\getGenderPhasePercentage{f}{1}\%
% Second-period total row: change only the final argument.
\getGenderPhaseTotal{f}{2}
\getGenderPhasePercentage{f}{2}\%
\end{dispListing}
\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-person-counts-and-percentages-for-a-funding-period}]{Person counts and percentages for a funding period}
W3 professors in period 3:
\getPositionPhaseTotal{prof-W3}{3}.\par
Women among them:
\getPositionGenderPhaseAmount{prof-W3}{f}{3}
(\getPositionGenderPhasePercentage{prof-W3}{f}{3}\%).\par
Women across counted positions:
\getGenderPhaseTotal{f}{3}
(\getGenderPhasePercentage{f}{3}\%).
\end{dfgExample}

