\subsection{Refer to financial totals in prose}

The near-identical funding names encode two different scopes.  Commands using
the singular name |RequestedFunding| use the proposal-wide
|RequestedFundingOverview| database by default.  Commands using the plural
name |RequestedFunds| use the project-level |RequestedFunding| database and
therefore require a project id.  In the signatures below,
\meta{sum-fields} means one or more fields such as |sum-year1| through
|sum-year5|.

\begin{description}
  \item[\cs{getCountRequestedStaff}]
    \oarg{project}\marg{year}\marg{category} prints the requested staff
    quantity, optionally restricted to one project.
    See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-requested-positions-and-multi-year-direct-costs}.

  \item[\cs{getSumRequestedFunding}]
    \oarg{database}\marg{year}\marg{type} prints the proposal-wide total for
    one funding type; use |year=all| for all five years.
    See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-project-counts-and-funding-totals}.

  \item[\cs{getTotalSumRequestedFunding}]
    \oarg{database}\marg{year}\marg{type} has the same proposal-wide scope and
    additionally accepts |type=all| for the grand total.
    See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-project-counts-and-funding-totals}.

  \item[\cs{getSumPreviousRequestedFunding}]
    \oarg{database}\marg{year}\marg{fields} totals the selected columns in
    the previous-period database; |year=all| includes every row.
  \item[\cs{getSumRequestedFunds}]
    \oarg{database}\marg{project}\marg{type}\marg{sum-fields} prints a
    project total for one funding type.
    See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-project-counts-and-funding-totals}.

  \item[\cs{getTotalSumRequestedFunds}]
    \oarg{database}\marg{project}\marg{sum-fields} prints a project total
    across all funding types.
    See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-project-counts-and-funding-totals}.

  \item[\cs{getSumRequestedFundsInstrumentation}]
    \oarg{project}\marg{sum-fields} prints the proposal-wide instrumentation
    total or restricts it to the optional project.
  \item[\cs{getSumRequestedFundingDirectCosts}]
    \oarg{database}\marg{year}\marg{group} totals direct costs for
    |applicant|, |participant|, |other-funds|, or |all|.
  \item[\cs{getSumExistingFunds}]
    \oarg{database}\marg{project}\marg{sum-fields} prints existing direct-cost
    funding for one project.
\end{description}

For example:
\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-project-counts-and-funding-totals}]{Project counts and funding totals}
Area A projects: \getProjectCount[A]\par
Staff: \getSumRequestedFunding{all}{staff}\par
Total: \getTotalSumRequestedFunding{all}{all}\par
A02 staff, year 1:
\getSumRequestedFunds{A02}{staff}{sum-year1}\par
A02 total, year 1:
\getTotalSumRequestedFunds{A02}{sum-year1}
\end{dfgExample}

Use the optional project argument to compare a project with the full request.
Here |1| is the first funding-year column; |PhD| is the staff category.
\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-requested-positions-and-multi-year-direct-costs}]{Requested positions and multi-year direct costs}
A02 doctoral positions, year 1:
\getCountRequestedStaff[A02]{1}{PhD}.\par
All doctoral positions, year 1:
\getCountRequestedStaff{1}{PhD}.\par
A02 direct costs, years 1 and 2:
\getSumRequestedFunds{A02}{costs}{sum-year1,sum-year2}\,EUR.
\end{dfgExample}

\section{Individual projects}
\label{sec:manual-project-status}
\subsection{Choose the structure from project status and type}
Set |project-status| in the project's metadata: |N| for new, |C| for continuing,
and |E| for ending. This is independent of |project-type|: |research|,
|service|, |mgk| (integrated research training), or |admin| (central coordination).
Changing metadata adjusts the generated heading and some tables; it does not
rewrite the authored sections. Start from the matching generated content file.

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-new-continuing-and-ending-project-structures},dfg wide]{New, continuing, and ending project structures}
\begin{TableEnv}{X[1,l] X[2,l] X[3,l]}[row{1}={font=\bfseries}]
  {Choose a project structure}{tab:manual-project-status}
Status & Background & Forward-looking content \\
N: new & Research rationale and preliminary work
  & Project plan, collaborations, and requested funding \\
C: continuing & Progress to date and current state of research
  & Project plan, collaborations, and requested funding \\
E: ending & Project history, report, and published results
  & Closing funding and staff from the ending period
\end{TableEnv}
\end{dfgExample}

The starter files |03_project_A02.tex| and |03_project_A01.tex| demonstrate
new and continuing research projects, respectively. Both have a summary,
aims, work packages, a timeline, the relevance of sex/gender/diversity,
the project's CRC role, differentiation from other grants, and project funding.
The ending example |03_project_A00.tex| instead contains a report, three result
categories, and closing funding information; it does not request a new period
or present a new work programme.

For service projects, |03_project_Q01.tex| uses the continuing structure but
focuses on services, access, capacity, and quality assurance. The training
example |03_project_Q02.tex| and coordination example |03_project_Q03.tex|
have dedicated content structures. Their headings, like ending-project
headings, omit the research-area line. A |reference-only=true| project is a
separate concept: it records a former project for references and is excluded
from active counts. The manual's |A00| demonstrates this case.

\subsection{Start and finish a project}
\begin{docCommand}{printProject}{\marg{specification}}
  Use the unified project-boundary interface.  Start a project with its id and
  finish the active project without repeating that id:
  \begin{dispListing}
    \printProject{{Head}{A01}}
    \ldots
    \printProject{{Tail}}
  \end{dispListing}
  The \texttt{Head} form delegates to \refCom{printProjectHead}; the
  \texttt{Tail} form delegates to \refCom{printProjectTail}.  The original
  commands remain available as compatibility fallbacks.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-new-project-heading-excerpt-from-the-starter-proposal}, \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-ending-project-heading-excerpt-from-the-starter-proposal}.
\end{docCommand}

\begin{docCommand}{printProjectHead}{\marg{project-id}}
  Start the section for one project.  The command sets the current project,
  creates the project heading, resets project-local counters, starts a
  bibliography refsection, prints the project leaders, and prepares the
  project-specific thumbindex and headers.  Active research and service
  projects also receive the DFG research-areas heading from their
  \refKey{research-area} metadata; ending, MGK, and administrative projects do
  not.  For every person id in the
  project's comma-separated \refKey{ref-person-id} value, all names stored in
  \refKey{name-alias} are registered as emphasized bibliography names.  An
  empty alias value is ignored.

  With \refKey{fastcompile-project}, the current-project state, counters,
  labels, a compact running header containing the project ID, and the optional
  bibliography refsection are prepared.  A light hook-based draft watermark is
  added to every shipped page; the calculated heading, project-leader cards,
  contract information, legal-issues table, thumb index, and table-of-contents
  entry are omitted.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-continuing-project-heading-excerpt-from-the-starter-proposal}.
\end{docCommand}

\begin{docCommand}{printProjectTail}{}
  Finish the current project section.  The command closes the bibliography
  refsection, resets bold bibliography names, restores the table-of-contents
  depth, and starts a new page.  In \refKey{fastcompile-project}, it only closes
  the optional publication refsection and does not force a page break.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-continuing-project-heading-excerpt-from-the-starter-proposal}.
\end{docCommand}

These commands manage complete pages and bibliography sections. The following
code/result examples therefore show the beginning of the actual example
proposal pages, rather than running a project wrapper inside the manual's
own section. The complete proposal and inline examples share the same
metadata. The omitted project body belongs between the two commands.
Former project |A00| illustrates the ending-project structure; it remains
excluded from active project counts and funding requests.

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-new-project-heading-excerpt-from-the-starter-proposal},dfg wide,listing and comment,
  comment={\dfgDocProjectExcerpt{A02}{200mm}}]{New project: heading excerpt from the example proposal}
% In project metadata: project-status = {N}
\printProject{{Head}{A02}}
% Summary, research rationale, plan, and funding.
\printProject{{Tail}}
\end{dfgExample}

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-continuing-project-heading-excerpt-from-the-starter-proposal},dfg wide,listing and comment,
  comment={\dfgDocProjectExcerpt{A01}{200mm}}]{Continuing project: heading excerpt from the example proposal}
% In project metadata: project-status = {C}
\printProjectHead{A01}
% Summary, progress to date, plan, and funding.
\printProjectTail
\end{dfgExample}

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-ending-project-heading-excerpt-from-the-starter-proposal},dfg wide,listing and comment,
  comment={\dfgDocProjectExcerpt{A00}{218mm}}]{Ending project: heading excerpt from the example proposal}
% In project metadata: project-status = {E}
\printProject{{Head}{A00}}
% Project history, published results, closing funding.
\printProject{{Tail}}
\end{dfgExample}

\subsection{Publications for new and continuing projects}
\begin{docCommand}{projectPublicationsHeading}{}
  Print the standard project-publications subsection heading only when
  \refKey{fastcompile-project-show-publications} is enabled.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-project-publications-with-the-standard-heading}.
\end{docCommand}

\begin{docCommand}{projectNocite}{\marg{entry keys}}
  Add project publication entries with \cs{nocite} only when
  \refKey{fastcompile-project-show-publications} is enabled.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-project-publications-with-the-standard-heading}.
\end{docCommand}

\begin{docCommand}{printProjectBibliography}{\oarg{biblatex options}}
  Print a project bibliography only when \refKey{fastcompile-project-show-publications} is
  enabled.  Use these three project-specific commands together so disabling
  publication output also removes its heading and explicit bibliography work.
  Project bibliography entries are typeset in \cs{footnotesize}.  With
  \refKey{emphasizeEntries} enabled, an entry carrying the current project's
  \texttt{<project-id>-highlight} keyword is printed in bold.  A direct
  \cs{printbibliography} call bypasses this formatting, the font setting,
  and the publication-output switch.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-project-publications-with-the-standard-heading}.
\end{docCommand}

A local |refsection| keeps this example's bibliography independent of the
manual. In an ordinary project, use the reference section established by the
project wrapper. The leading options are passed to |biblatex|.
\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-project-publications-with-the-standard-heading},dfg wide,dfg project=A02]{Project publications with the standard heading}
\begin{refsection}
\projectPublicationsHeading
\projectNocite{FAIR2016}
\printProjectBibliography[heading=none]
\end{refsection}
\end{dfgExample}

\subsection{Project plan: aims and work packages}

For writing aims and workpackages we provide some special commands and features, including a gantt-chart.

\begin{docEnvironment}{aims}{\oarg{options}}
  Environment to be used in a project section to summarize in a condensed way the aims of the project.
  Use |label={RDM Aims}| to replace the default |Aim| prefix, keeping the
  item number and colon. The same text is used verbatim for singular and
  plural references through \cs{cref}, \cs{Cref}, and \refCom{getAim},
  including references outside the list or before it. Compile twice after
  changing labels. Without this option, the defaults remain |Aim| and |Aims|.
  Other list options, such as |start=3| and |itemsep=2pt|, are passed to
  \pkg{enumitem}.

  Use |resume| to continue numbering from the previous |aims| list.
  Repeat the custom prefix, as in |[label={RDM Aims},resume]|, to keep it
  in the numbered items and their cross-references. With |[resume]| alone,
  numbering continues but the prefix returns to the default |Aim|/|Aims|.
  Resuming uses the previous |aims| list regardless of its label text;
  custom labels do not create separate counters.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-aims-and-their-references}.
\end{docEnvironment}

\begin{docCommand}{addAim}{\marg{label}}
  Add an aim within the \refEnv{aims} environment. You can refer to this aim by using \refCom{getAim}\marg{label}.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-aims-and-their-references}.
\end{docCommand}

These aim and work-package examples use project |A03| as their current
project, as normally established by \cs{printProjectHead}.

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-aims-and-their-references},dfg project=A03]{Aims and their references}
\begin{aims}
  \addAim{manual-a} Identify pathways.
  \addAim{manual-b} Validate biomarkers.
  \addAim{manual-c} Test interventions.
\end{aims}
See \getAim{manual-a,manual-b,manual-c}.
\begin{aims}[label={RDM Aims}]
  \addAim{manual-rdm-a} Document research data.
  \addAim{manual-rdm-b} Publish reusable datasets.
\end{aims}
See \getAim{manual-rdm-a,manual-rdm-b}.
\begin{aims}[label={RDM Aims},resume]
  \addAim{manual-rdm-c} Archive the datasets.
\end{aims}
Continued as \getAim{manual-rdm-c}.
\end{dfgExample}

\begin{docCommand}{getAim}{\marg{label}}
  Refer to an aim with the \marg{label} in the \refEnv{aims} environment.
  You can refer to multiple aims, too: |\getAim{A,B,C}|.

  The default is referencing the aims of the same project.
  You can also refer to an aim of a different project:
  \cs{getAim}\oarg{project-id}\marg{label}.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-aims-and-their-references}, \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-references-to-another-project-s-work-packages}.
\end{docCommand}


\begin{docEnvironment}{workpackages}{}
  Environment to be used in a project section when workpackages are written.
  This environment contains all descriptions of the workpackages that are introduced by
  \refCom{addWP}.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-work-packages-and-their-references}, \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-nested-work-packages-and-mixed-references}.
\end{docEnvironment}

\begin{docCommand}{addWP}{\marg{label}\marg{key=values}}
  Add a workpackage with the \marg{label} to the list.
  Further specifications are done using \refKey{name},
  \refKey{description} (text is used for the Gantt chart printed with
  \refCom{printPlot}),
  \refKey{periodStart},
  \refKey{periodEnd},
  \refKey{style}.
  At the top level, the \refKey{name} value is printed as the bold work-package
  heading.  Sub-workpackages do not print a \refKey{name}; their authored prose
  starts directly after the list label.  Immediate source whitespace, including
  a blank line after \cs{addWP}, is consumed without inserting another space.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-work-packages-and-their-references}, \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-nested-work-packages-and-mixed-references}.
\end{docCommand}

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-work-packages-and-their-references},dfg project=A03]{Work packages and their references}
\begin{workpackages}
  \addWP{manual-wp1}{
    name={Identify pathways},
    description={Pathway discovery},
    periodStart=1, periodEnd=6,
    style={alternative}
  }
  Collect and analyse samples.
  \addWP{manual-wp2}{
    name={Validate biomarkers},
    description={Validation},
    periodStart=7, periodEnd=12,
    style={important}
  }
  Test independent cohorts.
\end{workpackages}
See \getWP{manual-wp1,manual-wp2}.
\end{dfgExample}


Furthermore, PI, work-package, and aim references each have an independent
color setting. They default to |black| and are independent of
\refKey{color@reference@boldAuthor}. Configure them with \cs{changeSettings}:
\begin{dispListing}
  \changeSettings{
    color@reference@PI  = my-blue,
    color@reference@WP  = my-blue,
    color@reference@Aim = my-blue
  }
\end{dispListing}

\begin{docCommand}{getWP}{\marg{label}}
  Refer to a workpackage with the \marg{label} in the \refEnv{workpackages} environment.
  You can refer to multiple workpackages, too: |\getWP{wp2,wp3,wp4}|.
  Consecutive references under the same parent work package are compacted into
  a range.  Its |to| connector uses the surrounding font weight while the
  |WPs| prefix and endpoint labels stay bold. A narrow, unbreakable space
  separates |WP| or |WPs| from its number. References under later parent
  work packages remain separate and are not discarded during compaction.

  The default is referencing the workpackage of the same project.
  You can also refer to a workpackage of a different project:
  \cs{getWP}\oarg{project-id}\marg{label}.
  Parent and nested work packages can be combined in one call, for example
  |\getWP{wp1a,wp1b,wp2,wp3a,wp3b}|. The parent |wp2| is retained alongside
  the nested items; sorting follows the hierarchy and ranges do not cross
  parent boundaries.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-work-packages-and-their-references}, \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-nested-work-packages-and-mixed-references}.
\end{docCommand}

For this example the current project is |B04|. Parent and nested work packages
can be referenced together; nested ranges stay within their parent.
\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-nested-work-packages-and-mixed-references},dfg wide,dfg project=B04,leftlower=12mm]{Nested work packages and mixed references}
\begin{workpackages}
  \addWP{manual-nested-1}{name={Discovery}}
  \begin{workpackages}
    \addWP{manual-nested-1a}{} Recruit participants.
    \addWP{manual-nested-1b}{} Profile samples.
    \addWP{manual-nested-1c}{} Validate signals.
  \end{workpackages}
  \addWP{manual-nested-2}{name={Translation}}
  Test the selected intervention.
\end{workpackages}
See \getWP{manual-nested-1a,manual-nested-1b,manual-nested-1c,manual-nested-2}.
\end{dfgExample}

The next example is in |A03|. Its explicit |[B04]| argument points back to
the work packages above, while |[A03]| selects the earlier aim example.
\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-references-to-another-project-s-work-packages},dfg project=A03]{References to another project's work packages}
Exchange samples with
\getWP[B04]{manual-nested-1a,manual-nested-2}.\par
This supports \getAim[A03]{manual-a,manual-c}.
\end{dfgExample}






While some keys have been described already, here is what is missing so far:
\begin{docKey}{style}{\docKeyChoices{default,alternative,crosshatch,important,striped}}{initially: \texttt{default}}

  Define the style of the bar of this workpackage in the gantt-chart.

  For customizing the colors of the various styles have a look at:
  \refKey{color@gantt@default},
  \refKey{color@gantt@alternative},
  \refKey{color@gantt@crosshatch},
  \refKey{color@gantt@important},
  \refKey{color@gantt@stripe-one} and
  \refKey{color@gantt@stripe-two}.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-gantt-five-bar-styles},
  \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-gantt-custom-colours}, and
  \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-gantt-grayscale}.
\end{docKey}

\subsection{Timeline and collaboration plots}
\begin{docCommand}{printPlot}{\oarg{options}\marg{plot specification}}
  Print a Gantt or collaboration plot.  Use the lowercase type names
  |gantt| and |collaborations| in the braced specification.  The second
  braced item selects a project, as in |\printPlot{{gantt}{B06}}| or
  |\printPlot{{collaborations}{B06}}|.  Omit the project ID inside a project
  to use its current ID: |\printPlot{{gantt}}| or
  |\printPlot{{collaborations}}|.  Without a current project, give the ID.
  Place plot options before the specification, for example
  |\printPlot[arcs=true]{{collaborations}{B06}}| or
  |\printPlot[caption={Additional details}]{{gantt}}|.  Use the same leading
  caption option with an explicit ID, as in
  |\printPlot[caption={Additional details}]{{gantt}{B06}}|.
  Captions stay below plots; older trailing captions remain supported.
  For either plot type, an empty |caption={}| leaves the default caption intact
  and serves as a placeholder for additional project-specific text.
  The former type names |Gantt| and |ProjectCollaboration| and the singular
  lowercase |collaboration| remain accepted.

  The collaboration arc diagram is the default.  Select the heatmap with
  |layout=heatmap|.  Heatmap
  columns use a shared dynamic box width based on the project area with the
  most projects, and inactive no-collaboration projects have white backgrounds.
  Project areas are read from the optional |project-area| field of each
  |project| entry; if it is absent, the first character of the project ID is
  used.  Rows are created for every distinct area, ordered by area and project
  ID.  The familiar A, B, and Q colors are retained; other areas use a rotating
  palette.  Override individual colors with
  |area-colors={C=orange,D=violet}| or replace the fallback palette with
  |area-palette={teal,brown,magenta}| in the plot options.  Use
  \refKey{collaborationAreaColors} and
  \refKey{collaborationAreaPalette} in \refCom{changeSettings} to apply the
  same customization to every collaboration plot.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-gantt-chart-of-the-current-project}, \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-current-project-connections-as-arcs}, \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-explicit-project-heatmap-and-caption}.
  For bar colours and patterns, see
  \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-gantt-five-bar-styles} and
  \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-gantt-custom-colours}.
\end{docCommand}

\paragraph{Live plot variants}
\label{sec:manual-plot-examples}
For examples without an explicit ID, the current project is |A03|, as it
would be after |\printProjectHead{A03}|. Specifying |B04| overrides that
context for that call. Each example starts with the default plot options.

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-collaborations-of-the-current-project},dfg wide,dfg project=A03]{Collaborations of the current project}
\printPlot{{collaborations}}
\end{dfgExample}

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-explicit-id-overrides-the-current-project},dfg wide,dfg project=A03]{Explicit ID overrides the current project}
\printPlot{{collaborations}{B04}}
\end{dfgExample}

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-current-project-connections-as-arcs},dfg wide,dfg project=A03]{Current-project connections as arcs}
\printPlot[arcs=true]{{collaborations}}
\end{dfgExample}

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-current-project-heatmap},dfg wide,dfg project=A03]{Current-project heatmap}
\printPlot[layout=heatmap]{{collaborations}}
\end{dfgExample}

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-explicit-project-heatmap-and-caption},dfg wide,dfg project=A03]{Explicit-project heatmap and caption}
\printPlot[layout=heatmap,caption={Shared experimental platforms.}]
  {{collaborations}{B04}}
\end{dfgExample}

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-custom-project-area-colours},dfg wide,dfg project=A03]{Custom project-area colours}
\printPlot[layout=heatmap,area-colors={A=teal,B=orange,Q=violet}]
  {{collaborations}{A03}}
\end{dfgExample}

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-layout-aliases-and-additional-caption},dfg wide,dfg project=A03]{Layout aliases and additional caption}
\printPlot[arc-diagram,caption={Partners of the imaging service.}]
  {{collaborations}{Q01}}
\end{dfgExample}

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-gantt-chart-of-the-current-project},dfg wide,dfg project=A03]{Gantt chart of the current project}
\printPlot[caption={Discovery followed by validation.}]{{gantt}}
\end{dfgExample}

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-gantt-chart-with-explicit-project-id},dfg wide,dfg project=A03]{Gantt chart with explicit project ID}
\printPlot{{gantt}{A03}}
\end{dfgExample}

For older sources, |Gantt| means |gantt|; |ProjectCollaboration| and
|collaboration| mean |collaborations|. These aliases render the same results
and accept the same leading options.

\paragraph{Gantt bar styles and colours}
\label{sec:manual-gantt-colours}
Choose a bar's appearance with the \refKey{style} key of \refCom{addWP}.
The three solid fills are |default|, |alternative|, and |important|;
|crosshatch| adds a hatch pattern, and |striped| alternates two colours.
The following example defines one work package of each style in |B06|.
Its five rows are reused in the palette examples below, so the colours can
be compared without changing the work plan.

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-gantt-five-bar-styles},dfg wide,dfg project=B06,leftlower=12mm]{Five Gantt bar styles}
\begin{workpackages}
  \addWP{manual-colour-default}{
    name={Baseline}, description={Default},
    periodStart=1, periodEnd=6, style={default}
  }
  \addWP{manual-colour-alternative}{
    name={Parallel analysis}, description={Alternative},
    periodStart=3, periodEnd=9, style={alternative}
  }
  \addWP{manual-colour-important}{
    name={Validation}, description={Important},
    periodStart=7, periodEnd=12, style={important}
  }
  \addWP{manual-colour-crosshatch}{
    name={Integration}, description={Crosshatch},
    periodStart=9, periodEnd=14, style={crosshatch}
  }
  \addWP{manual-colour-striped}{
    name={Joint experiments}, description={Striped},
    periodStart=11, periodEnd=16, style={striped}
  }
\end{workpackages}
\printPlot[caption={One row for each built-in bar style.}]{{gantt}{B06}}
\end{dfgExample}

For a document-wide palette, put \cs{changeSettings} in
|preamble/dfg-customization.tex|. These colour settings are applied at
|\begin{document}|. They accept colour names and xcolor mixes such as
|teal!45|. The |frame| colour controls the outline of the bars, while
|crosshatch| controls the hatch strokes and |stripe-one|/|stripe-two|
control the two alternating stripe colours.
\begin{dispListing*}{title={Document-wide Gantt palette: place in the customization file}}
\changeSettings{
  color@gantt@default     = {teal!45},
  color@gantt@alternative = {orange!50},
  color@gantt@important   = {violet!70},
  color@gantt@crosshatch  = {black!25},
  color@gantt@stripe-one  = {teal!20},
  color@gantt@stripe-two  = {teal!80},
  color@gantt@frame       = {black!50}
}
\end{dispListing*}

To recolour just one chart after the document has started, use
\cs{colorlet} on the live colour names inside a group. The next example
also defines an RGB colour; the surrounding group restores the previous
palette afterwards. The colour names are set before \refCom{printPlot};
they are not plot options.

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-gantt-custom-colours},dfg wide,dfg project=B06]{Custom colours for one Gantt chart}
\begingroup
\definecolor{ganttOcean}{RGB}{0,114,178}
\colorlet{color@gantt@default}{ganttOcean!45}
\colorlet{color@gantt@alternative}{teal!50}
\colorlet{color@gantt@important}{orange!90}
\colorlet{color@gantt@crosshatch}{violet!35}
\colorlet{color@gantt@stripe-one}{ganttOcean!25}
\colorlet{color@gantt@stripe-two}{ganttOcean!55}
\colorlet{color@gantt@frame}{black!50}
\printPlot[caption={Custom fills, coloured hatching, and two-tone stripes.}]
  {{gantt}{B06}}
\endgroup
\end{dfgExample}

For grayscale output, use different gray levels and retain the hatch and
stripe patterns. The following chart uses the same work packages and dates;
its black outlines make the bar boundaries explicit.

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-gantt-grayscale},dfg wide,dfg project=B06]{Grayscale Gantt bars with patterns}
\begingroup
\colorlet{color@gantt@default}{black!20}
\colorlet{color@gantt@alternative}{black!35}
\colorlet{color@gantt@important}{black!50}
\colorlet{color@gantt@crosshatch}{black!25}
\colorlet{color@gantt@stripe-one}{white}
\colorlet{color@gantt@stripe-two}{black!35}
\colorlet{color@gantt@frame}{black}
\printPlot[caption={Grayscale fills with hatching and stripes.}]{{gantt}{B06}}
\endgroup
\end{dfgExample}

\subsection{Individual project funding}
\label{sec:manual-project-funding}
The example below adds a funding request for |A06|, whose project metadata
are already loaded. It covers all five funding groups: |staff|, |costs|,
|fellowships|, |globalfunds|, and |instrumentation|. The doctoral position at
65\% uses the configured annual salary; the other groups specify full EUR
amounts directly, including a one-off equipment purchase. The year headings
and partial-year factors come from |meta.fundingYears|. The earlier |A02|
funding examples use separate records.

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-requested-funding-for-a-project},dfg wide,listing above* text,after={\par\clearpage}]{Requested funding for a project}
\changeSettings{salary@PhD=83700}
\addEntry[funding]{RequestedFunding}{
  ref-project-id = {A06},
  funding-type   = {staff},
  category       = {PhD},
  percentage     = {65},
  quantity       = {
    year1 = {1},
    year2 = {1},
    year3 = {1},
    year4 = {1},
    year5 = {1}
  }
}
\addEntry[funding]{RequestedFunding}{
  ref-project-id = {A06},
  funding-type   = {costs},
  category       = {consumables},
  sum            = {
    year1 = {1000},
    year2 = {2000},
    year3 = {2000},
    year4 = {2000},
    year5 = {1000}
  }
}
\addEntry[funding]{RequestedFunding}{
  ref-project-id = {A06},
  funding-type   = {fellowships},
  category       = {Short-term research fellowships},
  sum            = {
    year1 = {3000},
    year2 = {6000},
    year3 = {6000},
    year4 = {6000},
    year5 = {3000}
  }
}
\addEntry[funding]{RequestedFunding}{
  ref-project-id = {A06},
  funding-type   = {globalfunds},
  category       = {Training and networking},
  sum            = {
    year1 = {1500},
    year2 = {3000},
    year3 = {3000},
    year4 = {3000},
    year5 = {1500}
  }
}
\addEntry[funding]{RequestedFunding}{
  ref-project-id = {A06},
  funding-type   = {instrumentation},
