}
\printTable{{RequestedFunding}{A06}}
\end{dfgExample}

The project staff roster describes who does the work. It uses |staff| records
in |RequestedFundingStaff|; monetary requests remain separate |funding|
records. Existing staff may inherit their name and institution through
|ref-person-id|. Use |lastname={TBA}| for an unnamed requested position.
The |description| text is printed as a numbered job description below the table.

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-project-staff-an-existing-investigator-and-a-doctoral-vacancy},dfg wide,listing above* text]{Project staff: an existing investigator and a doctoral vacancy}
\addEntry[staff]{RequestedFundingStaff}{
  ref-project-id    = {B03},
  funding-status    = {existing},
  staff-type        = {research},
  ref-person-id     = {per11},
  field-of-research = {Immunology},
  commitment        = {8},
  funding-source    = {State funds},
  description       = {Supervises the experiments and data analysis.}
}
\addEntry[staff]{RequestedFundingStaff}{
  ref-project-id     = {B03},
  funding-status     = {requested},
  staff-type         = {research},
  lastname           = {TBA},
  positionText       = {Doctoral researcher (65\%)},
  field-of-research  = {Immunology},
  ref-institution-id = {ins1},
  category           = {PhD},
  description        = {Performs longitudinal experiments and prepares the thesis.}
}
\printTable{{RequestedFundingStaff}{B03}}
\end{dfgExample}

Existing direct-cost funding is declared separately from new requests. The
following records identify the participating affiliations and funding sources;
the |sum| values are full EUR amounts for each funding-year column.

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-existing-direct-cost-funding-from-two-sources},dfg wide,listing above* text]{Existing direct-cost funding from two sources}
\addEntry[funding]{RequestedAndExistingFundingDirectCosts}{
  ref-project-id     = {B03},
  ref-affiliation-id = {aff1},
  financed-by        = {University core funds},
  sum                = {
    year1 = {1000}, year2 = {2000}, year3 = {2000}, year4 = {2000}, year5 = {1000}
  }
}
\addEntry[funding]{RequestedAndExistingFundingDirectCosts}{
  ref-project-id     = {B03},
  ref-affiliation-id = {aff2},
  financed-by        = {Partner university},
  sum                = {
    year1 = {500}, year2 = {1000}, year3 = {1000}, year4 = {1000}, year5 = {500}
  }
}
\printTable{{RequestedAndExistingFundingDirectCosts}{B03}}
\end{dfgExample}

\begin{docEnvironment}{FundingDirectCostsTable}{\oarg{heading}}
  Typeset a three-column breakdown of direct costs.  Its optional heading and
  the table stay together when there is insufficient space left on a page.
  The currency column uses its natural width so that codes such as EUR fit
  in the selected font; the description column fills the remaining width.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-explain-individual-direct-cost-items}.
\end{docEnvironment}

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-explain-individual-direct-cost-items}]{Explain individual direct-cost items}
\begin{FundingDirectCostsTable}[Consumables]
  Assay reagents & EUR & 1200 \\
  Culture media & EUR & 800 \\
\end{FundingDirectCostsTable}
\end{dfgExample}

\subsection{Report and results of an ending project}
\begin{docCommand}{printProjectResults}{\marg{key-value list}}
  Print the three result categories required for an ending project.  The keys
  \texttt{quality-assured}, \texttt{other}, and \texttt{patents} each accept a
  comma-separated list of bibliography entry keys.  Use the literal value
  \texttt{none} when a category produced no results.  An omitted or empty key
  retains a drafting instruction instead, so an unfinished list is not
  accidentally reported as empty.

  Every non-empty category is placed in a separate \pkg{biblatex}
  reference segment within the reference section opened by
  \cs{printProjectHead}.  Consequently, category membership is local to the
  current project.  The command also places any pending floats before the
  result lists.

  The combined result-key list is written to the project's auxiliary file.
  On the next normal \LaTeX{} pass, a \texttt{projectReport} bibliography uses
  this list to omit report citations that are printed later as project
  results.  This follows the usual \pkg{biblatex} compilation cycle and does
  not require the entry keys to be duplicated in the report.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-published-results-of-an-ending-project}.
\end{docCommand}

\begin{docEnvironment}{projectReport}{}
  Isolate all citations in the project report in their own reference segment
  and print that segment's bibliography at the end of the environment.  Any
  entries assigned to \cs{printProjectResults} for the same project are
  omitted, because they are printed in the later result lists.  Place the
  environment immediately after the report subsection heading and label.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-published-results-of-an-ending-project}.
\end{docEnvironment}

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-published-results-of-an-ending-project},dfg wide,dfg project=A00]{Published results of an ending project}
\begin{refsection}
\begin{projectReport}
  Follow the FAIR principles \cite{FAIR2016}
  and document software design \cite{Hoare1973}.
\end{projectReport}
\printProjectResults{
  quality-assured={FAIR2016},
  other=none,
  patents=none
}
\end{refsection}
\end{dfgExample}

For |project-status=E|, the staff table reuses
|\printTable{{RequestedFundingStaff}{project-id}}|. Rows with
|funding-status=requested| describe staff funded with approved grant money
in the ending period, rather than a request for a new period. The complete
B02 example shows this closing table together with its input records.

\section{Bylaws and declarations}
The bylaws are authored prose in |content/04_bylaws-of-the-crc.tex|. Use the
applicant-wording helper introduced with the title page for singular or plural
university names. The declaration file |content/05_declarations.tex| then
prints the required signatures from the same person metadata.

\begin{docCommand}{printSignature}{\oarg{database}\marg{id-description-pairs}}
  Print one signature block for each \marg{id}\marg{description} pair.  The
  \meta{database} defaults to \texttt{person}.  Inside \meta{description},
  \cs{theAffiliationOfRoleID} contains the person's affiliation id and can be
  used for further database lookups.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-signature-blocks}.
\end{docCommand}

\begin{docCommand}{printSignaturesWithRole}{\oarg{database}\marg{role}\marg{description}}
  Print a signature block for every current-period person with \meta{role}.
  This is used for the rector, chancellor, or president of every applicant
  university, so a proposal with several applicants does not require a
  hard-coded list of person ids.  Inside \meta{description},
  \cs{theAffiliationOfRoleID} refers to the affiliation of the current
  signatory.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-select-signatories-by-role}.
\end{docCommand}

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-signature-blocks},dfg wide]{Signature blocks}
\printSignature{
  {spokesperson}{(Spokesperson of \thisCRC)}
  {rector}{(Rector of Example University)}
}
\end{dfgExample}

The role-based form resolves the affiliation for each signatory; it avoids
repeating names or maintaining a separate list of rectors in the content.
\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-select-signatories-by-role},dfg wide]{Select signatories by role}
\printSignaturesWithRole{rector}{%
  Rector of
  \get[affiliation]{\theAffiliationOfRoleID}{name}}
\end{dfgExample}

\section{Back matter: compact project overview}
The closing overview reuses the project and PI records already used in general
information. No separate database is needed.

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-compact-project-overview-for-the-closing-pages},dfg wide,listing above* text]{Compact project overview for the closing pages}
\printTable{projectOverview}
\end{dfgExample}

\chapter{Metadata and configuration reference}
\label{chap:manual-reference}
This chapter collects the detailed field definitions, class settings, CV and
bibliography conventions, and maintenance notes. Read it as a reference while
working through the preceding proposal examples.

\section{Metadata, databases and keysets}
\label{doc:metadata}
This reference lists the accepted fields for each keyset. The walkthrough
introduces the records at their point of use; consult this section when adding
fields or choosing a non-default value. The class stores records with
\refPkg{datatool}.

\paragraph{Databases}

We have these databases that are used and picked up by several commands by
default:
\begin{description}
  \item[meta] All the metainformation about the proposal
  \item[person] Everything that is related to a person
  \item[project] Anything that is related to a project
  \item[institution] Info about an institution, that is linked to:
  \item[affiliation] storing info about the affiliation
\end{description}
There are also more databases created on the fly but mainly used for project
specific purposes.
The examples below use one coherent sample dataset: \texttt{MM} and
\texttt{KM} are persons, \texttt{A01} is a project, \texttt{IAIS} is an
institution, and \texttt{RWTH} is the related affiliation.
\subsection{Keyset \textbf{person}}
\begin{dispListing*}{title={Example keyset to describe a person-entity.}}
(*\refCom{addEntry}*){person}{
  (*\refKey{id}*)                 = {MM},
  (*\refKey{firstname}*)          = {Max S.},
  (*\refKey{lastname}*)           = {Doe},
  (*\refKey{academicdegree}*)     = {Prof. Dr. rer. nat.},
  (*\refKey{gender}*)             = {m},
  (*\refKey{ref-institution-id}*) = {IAIS},
  (*\refKey{ref-project-id}*)     = {A01},
  (*\refKey{role}*)               = {PI,spokesperson},
  (*\refKey{position}*)           = {prof-W3},
  (*\refKey{positionText}*)       = {Professor of Microbial Systems},
  (*\refKey{period}*)             = {1},
  (*\refKey{name-alias}*)         = {{Max Doe},{M. S. Doe}},
  (*\refKey{orcid}*)              = {0000-0002-3555-7218},
  (*\refKey{email}*)              = {max.doe@example.edu},
  (*\refKey{phone}*)              = {+49 241 80 12345},
  (*\refKey{url}*)                = {https://example.edu/mm},
  (*\refKey{doctorateYear}*)      = {1995},
  (*\refKey{doctorate}*)          = {Dr. rer. nat.},
  (*\refKey{financed-by}*)        = {RWTH},
  (*\refKey{topic}*)              = {Host--microbiome interactions},
  (*\refKey{category}*)           = {prof},
  (*\refKey{dateDefense}*)        = {1995},
  (*\refKey{periodStart}*)        = {2025},
  (*\refKey{periodEnd}*)          = {2029},
  (*\refKey{contract-information}*) = {
    (*\refKey{C1}*)  = {yes},
    (*\refKey{C2}*)  = {no},
    (*\refKey{C3}*)  = {no},
    (*\refKey{C4}*)  = {yes},
  }
}
\end{dispListing*}
There are several keys you can and should use regarding a person.
They are described further done as an example on how to use \refCom{addEntry}
etc.
\begin{docKey}{email}{=\meta{string}}{}
Provide the email-address of the person.
\end{docKey}
\begin{docKey}{url}{=\meta{url}}{ }
URL of the persons academic/professional homepage (e.g. institute).
\end{docKey}

\begin{docKey}{phone}{=\meta{string}}{ }
Provide the phone number of the person.
\end{docKey}

\begin{docKey}{financed-by}{=\meta{string}}{ }
Describe the source of funding for the person’s e.g. position etc.
\end{docKey}

\begin{docKey}{topic}{=\meta{string}}{ }
Especially for the early career researchers describe their topic of research.
\end{docKey}

\begin{docKey}{category}{\docKeyChoices{groupLeader, prof , postDoc,medDoc,%
    PhD,research,nonResearch,student,assistant}}{ }
\refKey{category} is used in various databases.
Mostly it is used to state the category of the person, but it is also used
then for the early career researchers.
\end{docKey}

\begin{docKey}{contract-information}{=\meta{key-values}}{}
Provide information about the person's contractual situation.
The \meta{key-value} is further specified with
\refKey{C1},
\refKey{C2},
\refKey{C2a},
\refKey{C2b},
\refKey{C3},
\refKey{C3a},
\refKey{C4},
\refKey{C4a}.
The generated wording for the yes/no keys \refKey{C1}--\refKey{C4} is
singular/plural sensitive.  It is adjusted automatically to match the number
of project leaders and institutions represented in the proposal.
\end{docKey}

\begin{docKey}{C1}{\docKeyChoices{yes,no}}{}
This key collects the answer to \emph{Is the employment of the project leader(s) at the institution(s) indicated contractually secured for the duration of the proposed funding period?}
\end{docKey}
\begin{docKey}{C2}{\docKeyChoices{yes,no}}{}
This key collects the answer to \emph{Do any of the above mentioned persons hold fixed-term positions?}
\end{docKey}

\begin{docKey}{C2a}{=\meta{YYYY-MM-DD}}{}
If key \refKey{C2} has been answered with \meta{yes}, this question \emph{End
date of fixed-term contract:\meta{YYYY-MM-DD} } requires \meta{YYYY-MM-DD} as
answer, too.
\end{docKey}
\begin{docKey}{C2b}{\meta{YYYY-MM-DD}}{}
If key \refKey{C2} has been answered with \meta{yes}, this question
\emph{Further employment is planned until \meta{YYYY-MM-DD}} requires
\meta{YYYY-MM-DD} as answer, too.
\end{docKey}
\begin{docKey}{C3}{\docKeyChoices{yes,no}}{}
This key collects the answer to \emph{Will one or more of the above-mentioned persons retire during the proposed funding period?}.
\end{docKey}
\begin{docKey}{C3a}{=\meta{YYYY-MM-DD}}{}
If key \refKey{C3} has been answered with \meta{yes}, this question
\emph{anticipated retirement date: \meta{YYYY-MM-DD} } requires \meta{YYYY-MM-DD} as
answer, too.
\end{docKey}

\begin{docKey}{C4}{\docKeyChoices{yes,no}}{}
This key collects the answer to \emph{Funding for the position(s) of the project leader(s) at the institution(s) indicated is covered by core support (state funds or similar):}
\end{docKey}
\begin{docKey}{C4a}{=\meta{string}}{}
If key \refKey{C4} has been answered with \meta{no},
this key provides type and source of funding.
\end{docKey}

Following keys are mainly used for the CV-section but are closely tight to the person:
\begin{docKey}{orcid}{=\meta{0000-0000-0000-0000}}{}
  The individual string with the ORCiD.
  This value is also required for \refCom{getORCiD}.
\end{docKey}
\begin{docKey}{name-alias}{=\meta{string}}{ }
  More versions of the person's name.
  The various aliases for \refKey{name-alias} are also described under
  \refCom{addboldnames}.
\end{docKey}
\begin{docKeys}
{
  {doc name = doctorateYear,
    doc parameter = {=\meta{YYYY}}},
  {doc name = doctorate,
    doc parameter = {=\meta{string}}},
  {doc name = degree-programme,
    doc parameter = {=\meta{string}}},
}
Providing information about the doctorate.
\end{docKeys}



\subsection{Database entries for a \textbf{cv}}
\label{sec:doc:cv-basic}
The header of the CV section is (partly) based on the information you parse to
the document using the \refCom{addEntry} command with the keyset \textbf{person}:
Keys like \refKey{name-alias}, \refKey{orcid},
\refKey{doctorate}, \refKey{degree-programme},
\refKey{url} are used especially for the CV-section.

Let’s assume we have a person with the following information:

\begin{dispListing*}{title={Partly person-entity dataset focussing on its CV-entry.}}
  (*\refCom{addEntry}*){person}{
    (*\refKey{id}*)             ={MM},
    (*\refKey{firstname}*)      ={Max S.},
    (*\refKey{lastname}*)       ={Doe},
    (*\refKey{academicdegree}*) ={Prof. Dr. rer. nat.},
    (*\refKey{role}*)           ={PI},
    \ldots
    (*\refKey{name-alias}*)       ={{Maxi Doe},{Maximilian Doe}},
    (*\refKey{orcid}*)            ={0000-0002-3555-7218},
    (*\refKey{doctorateYear}*)    ={1995},
    (*\refKey{doctorate}*)        ={Dr. rer. nat.},
    (*\refKey{degree-programme}*) ={Biology},
    (*\refKey{url}*)              ={https://example.edu/mm},
  }
\end{dispListing*}


This set of information is unique to a person and therefore only needs to be
parsed once.

In the cv we need more dedicated information about the person. This is done
with a separate keyset so it will not clutter the person-entry too much.
You can have multiple of the following entries below for a person.
Most important stages should be indicated with |cv-type=mandatory|.
If you state |cv-type=supplement| you only need to
use the key |description|, same goes for |cv-type=activities| which will then be
in a different section, and last |cv-type=supervision|.
There are more sections with further information about the PI.
All the information go to the database \meta{cv}.

\begin{docKey}{ref-person-id}{=\meta{id}}{}
  Use the \meta{id} of the person to whom this entry belongs to.
\end{docKey}

\begin{docKey}{description}{=\meta{string}}{}
  Further description of the current entry.
\end{docKey}


\begin{docKey}{cv-type}{\docKeyChoices{mandatory,supplement,
      activities,supervision,
      distinction,other}}{}
  Specifying the type of one CV entry with additional information.
  Each CV of a person needs to have a \meta{mandatory} entry
  (as part of \emph{Qualification and Career}).
\end{docKey}

\begin{docKeys}[doc parameter = {=\marg{date}}]
  {
    {
      doc name = periodStart,
      doc description = starting date
    },
    {
      doc name = periodEnd,
      doc description = ending date
    },
    {
      doc name = dateDefense,
      doc description = date of the defense of the thesis
    },
  }
  Specifying the time span of an event. The \meta{date} should be given in an IS8601-form.
\end{docKeys}

Some information comes from the block described above, but for the \emph{Stages} you
need to describe them separately using the keys
\refKey{ref-person-id},
\refKey{cv-type},
\refKey{periodStart},
\refKey{periodEnd},
\refKey{name},
\refKey{position},
\refKey{positionText},
\refKey{description},
\refKey{ref-institution-id}.


\begin{dispListing*}{title={Example dataset-entry for a mandatory person-cv-entity.}}
  (*\refCom{addEntry}*){cv}{
    (*\refKey{ref-person-id}*)      ={MM},
    (*\refKey{cv-type}*)        ={mandatory},
    (*\refKey{periodStart}*)        ={1995},
    (*\refKey{periodEnd}*)          ={1998},
    (*\refKey{position}*)            ={prof-W3},
    (*\refKey{positionText}*)           ={Professor of Microbial Systems},
    (*\refKey{description}*)        ={Established an independent research group on host--microbiome interactions.},
    (*\refKey{ref-institution-id}*) ={IAIS},
  }
\end{dispListing*}

For each stage on the career path repeat the
\cs{addEntry}\marg{cv}\marg{ \ldots, type={mandatory}, \ldots}-block.


There are five more blocks to which a researcher can provide information. For
each block and for each entry within one block you need to create an entry.

\begin{description}
  \item[supplement:]   Supplementary Career Information
  \item[activities:]   Activities in the Research System
  \item[supervision:]  Supervision of Researchers in Early Career Phases
  \item[distinction:]  Academic Distinctions
  \item[other:]        Other Information
\end{description}
Here is a more concrete example
\begin{dispListing*}{title={Example dataset-entry for a common CV-entity}}
  (*\refCom{addEntry}*){cv}{
    (*\refKey{ref-person-id}*)      ={MM},
    (*\refKey{cv-type}*)            ={supplement},
    (*\refKey{description}*)        ={Member of the university research council since 2024.},
  }
\end{dispListing*}

\begin{docCommand}{dfgCV}{\marg{person-id}}
  Print the complete CV for one person.  The command expects a dedicated
  \texttt{cv-}\meta{person-id} database and combines the header, mandatory
  career stages, optional CV sections, selected publications, and the data
  privacy statement.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-generate-the-cv-appendix-or-select-one-person}.
\end{docCommand}

\begin{docCommand}{dfgCVsection}{\oarg{database}}
  Print the CVs for all PIs in the current funding period.  The
  \meta{database} defaults to \texttt{person}; persons are sorted by lastname
  and firstname before their CVs are printed with \refCom{dfgCV}.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-generate-the-cv-appendix-or-select-one-person}.
\end{docCommand}

The starter CV loads person and career records from
|metadata/dfg-metadata.tex| and |metadata/dfg-metadata-cv.tex|. The following
example shows the first CV page produced by the complete appendix. Its first
PI is |per2| (Alex Example); selecting just that person produces the same CV.
The local proposal, CV appendix, and manual use the same twelve-PI consortium.
All pages, including the privacy statement, follow in
Chapter~\ref{chap:complete-example-cv}.

\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-generate-the-cv-appendix-or-select-one-person},dfg wide,listing and comment,
  comment={\dfgDocCVExcerpt{per2}}]{Generate the CV appendix or select one person}
\documentclass[english,cv,final=true]{dfg-crc}
\begin{document}
\dfgCVsection
% For one person, replace the line above with:
% \dfgCV{per2}
\end{document}
\end{dfgExample}

\subsection{Keyset \textbf{project}}
Following keys are required to describe a project.
\begin{dispListing*}{title=Example dataset-entry to describe a project-entity.}
  (*\refCom{addEntry}*){project}{
    (*\refKey{id}*) = {A01},
    (*\refKey{number}*) = {A01},
    (*\refKey{name}*)   = {Microbial metabolism in barrier tissues},
    (*\refKey{project-type}*)   = {research},
    (*\refKey{project-status}*) = {N},
    (*\refKey{project-area}*)   = {A},
    (*\refKey{consider-for-lists}*) = {true},
    (*\refKey{reference-only}*) = {false},
    (*\refKey{research-area}*) = {1.11-01,1.11-03},
    (*\refKey{ref-person-id}*) = {MM,KM},
    (*\refKey{collaborations}*) = {A02,B01,Q01},
    (*\refKey{legal-issues}*)  = {
      (*\refKey{L1}*)  = {yes},
      (*\refKey{L1a}*) = {yes},
      (*\refKey{L2}*)  = {no},
      (*\refKey{L2a}*) = {},
      (*\refKey{L3}*)  = {yes},
      (*\refKey{L4}*)  = {no},
      (*\refKey{L5}*)  = {no},
      (*\refKey{L5a}*) = {},
      (*\refKey{L6}*)  = {no},
      (*\refKey{L7}*)  = {no},
      (*\refKey{L7a}*) = {},
    }
  }
\end{dispListing*}

Other required keys are \refKey{id}, \refKey{name},
\refKey{number}, \refKey{ref-person-id}.

Within an \refCom{addEntry}|{project}{...}| entry, the
\refKey{ref-person-id} field is reserved exclusively for the person or persons
who are PIs of that project. Every person listed in this field is interpreted
as a project PI; no other person roles are inspected or considered. Persons
associated with the project only in another role must therefore not be included
in this field.

\begin{docKey}{project-type}{\docKeyChoices{research,service,mgk,admin}}{}
  Hereby you specify the type of the project.
  \meta{research} stands for a classical research project,
  \meta{service} is service project,
  \meta{mgk} is an integrated graduate college,
  \meta{admin} is an administrative project.
\end{docKey}

\begin{docKey}{project-status}{\docKeyChoices{N,E,C}}{}
  Define the status of the project, where \meta{N} means a \emph{new} project,
  \meta{E} stands for \emph{ending} and \meta{C} for \emph{continuing} project.
  The generated local proposal contains complete, separate source examples for
  all three statuses.  Continuing projects use \enquote{Project progress to
  date}, new projects use \enquote{Research rationale}, and ending projects use
  the shorter \enquote{Project history}, \enquote{Published project results},
  and \enquote{Funding} structure.  In every case \refCom{printProjectHead}
  supplies the preceding general-information section from metadata.
\end{docKey}

\begin{docKey}{project-area}{=\meta{area-id}}{}
  Assign the project to a collaboration-plot area such as \texttt{A},
  \texttt{B}, or \texttt{Q}.  Any number of area identifiers is supported.
  If the key is omitted or empty, the first character of the project ID is
  used for compatibility with existing metadata (for example, \texttt{A01}
  belongs to area \texttt{A}).
\end{docKey}

\begin{docKey}{consider-for-lists}{\docKeyChoices{true,false}}{}
  Control whether the project participates in automatically generated project
  lists, project overview tables, research-area aggregates, and project counts.
  The default is \texttt{true}.  Setting this key to \texttt{false} does not
  remove the database row, so the project remains available to explicit
  reference commands such as \refCom{getProject}.
\end{docKey}

\begin{docKey}{reference-only}{\docKeyChoices{true,false}}{}
  Declare that the project exists only so historical or external project ids
  can be referenced.  The default is \texttt{false}.  A reference-only project
  is printed without a hyperlink because it has no project section that could
  provide a link target.  In prose it receives the explicit
  \enquote{former project} indicator.  Generated tables and lists instead append
  a compact superscript asterisk and add a local \enquote{Former project} legend
  when at least one such project occurs.  This key is independent of
  \refKey{consider-for-lists}: an ending project with a real project section can
  be excluded from lists without becoming reference-only.
\end{docKey}

\begin{docKey}{research-area}{=\meta{ticklist}}{}
  Collect all research areas (according to the DFG list) in a comma-separated structure.
  Spaces around individual codes are ignored, e.g.\ |2.21-02, 2.22-31|.
\end{docKey}

\begin{docKey}{collaborations}{=\meta{project-id-list}}{}
  List the other CRC projects collaborating with this project.  Collaborations
  are treated as undirected and duplicate declarations are collapsed, so the
  reverse relation does not need to be repeated.  The project collaboration
  plot reads its edges from this field and its nodes from the same
  \texttt{project} database.  Its default arc layout shows all active projects
  on one axis and highlights the focus project and its direct collaborators.
  Set |arcs=true| to draw the connecting arcs, or select the optional
  heatmap layout.  Ending, reference-only,
  and |consider-for-lists=false| projects are omitted, as are edges to those
  intentionally excluded nodes.
  All arcs reach one common peak height, so their horizontal span does not imply
  collaboration strength or weight.  Project identifiers are printed inside
  the nodes, and in the arc layout each project-area rule spans its complete
  contiguous group of nodes.
\end{docKey}

\begin{docKey}{legal-issues}{=\meta{further key-values}}{}
  Provide information about the project's legal issues.
  The \meta{key-value} is further specified with
  \refKey{L1},
  \refKey{L1a},
  \refKey{L2},
  \refKey{L2a},
  \refKey{L3},
  \refKey{L4},
  \refKey{L5},
  \refKey{L5a}.
  \refKey{L6}.
  \refKey{L7}.
  \refKey{L7a}.
\end{docKey}

\begin{docKey}{L1}{\docKeyChoices{yes,no}}{}
  This key records whether the project requires compliance with
  discipline-specific research ethics guidelines.
\end{docKey}
\begin{docKey}{L1a}{\docKeyChoices{yes,no}}{}
  If key \refKey{L1} has been answered with \meta{yes}, this statement
  records whether a statement from the responsible ethics committee is
  required and will be obtained before funding starts.
\end{docKey}

\begin{docKey}{L2}{\docKeyChoices{yes,no}}{}
  This key collects the answer to \emph{clinical trials}
\end{docKey}
\begin{docKey}{L2a}{\docKeyChoices{yes,no}}{}
  If key \refKey{L2} has been answered with \meta{yes}, this statement
  \emph{A copy of the studies’ registration is included with the proposal} requires an answer, too.
\end{docKey}

\begin{docKey}{L3}{\docKeyChoices{yes,no}}{}
  This key collects the answer to \emph{experiments involving vertebrates}
\end{docKey}

\begin{docKey}{L4}{\docKeyChoices{yes,no}}{}
  This key collects the answer to \emph{experiments involving recombinant DNA}
\end{docKey}


\begin{docKey}{L5}{\docKeyChoices{yes,no}}{}
  This key collects the answer to \emph{research involving human embryonic stem cells}
\end{docKey}
\begin{docKey}{L5a}{\docKeyChoices{yes,no}}{}
  If key \refKey{L5} has been answered with \meta{yes}, this statement
  \emph{Legal authorization has been obtained} requires an answer, too.
\end{docKey}

\begin{docKey}{L6}{\docKeyChoices{yes,no}}{}
  This key collects the answer to \emph{research concerning the Convention on Biological Diversity}
\end{docKey}

\begin{docKey}{L7}{\docKeyChoices{yes,no}}{}
  This key collects the answer to \emph{investigations involving dual use research of concern}
\end{docKey}
\begin{docKey}{L7a}{\docKeyChoices{yes,no}}{}
  If key \refKey{L7} has been answered with \meta{yes}, this statement
  \emph{A copy of the statement by the institution’s research ethics committee is included with the proposal} requires an answer, too.
\end{docKey}

\subsection{Keyset \textbf{institution}}
An institution describes the organisational unit a person belongs to, for
example an institute, department, centre, or clinic.  It is linked to its parent
affiliation through \refKey{ref-affiliation-id}.  The \refKey{abbreviation}
is the compact, reader-facing label used in generated table cells; the internal
\refKey{id} is never intended as display text.  Full institution lists and
legends continue to use \refKey{name}.  For compatibility, a missing
\refKey{abbreviation} falls back to \refKey{name}.  The \refKey{name} key may
be left empty when the affiliation itself is the relevant institution-level
text; in that case institution-list commands start with the affiliation name.

\begin{dispListing*}{title=Example dataset-entry to describe an institution-entity.}
  (*\refCom{addEntry}*){institution}{
    (*\refKey{id}*) = {IAIS},
    (*\refKey{name}*) = {Institute for Applied Immunology and Systems Biology},
    (*\refKey{abbreviation}*) = {IAIS},
    (*\refKey{ref-affiliation-id}*) = {RWTH},
  }
\end{dispListing*}

\begin{dispListing*}{title={Institution without a separate institution name.}}
  (*\refCom{addEntry}*){institution}{
    (*\refKey{id}*) = {RWTH-main},
    (*\refKey{name}*) = {},
    (*\refKey{abbreviation}*) = {RWTH},
    (*\refKey{ref-affiliation-id}*) = {RWTH},
  }
\end{dispListing*}

\begin{dispListing*}{title={Person with multiple institutions.}}
  (*\refCom{addEntry}*){person}{
    (*\refKey{id}*) = {MM},
    (*\refKey{firstname}*) = {Maria},
    (*\refKey{lastname}*) = {Sample},
    (*\refKey{ref-institution-id}*) = {IAIS,RWTH-main},
  }
\end{dispListing*}

In standard institution lists, multiple institutions are printed as separate
paragraphs with a small vertical gap.  In \texttt{narrow} mode, they are joined
inline with semicolons.  The \texttt{abbreviation} option selects institution
abbreviations (with a full-name fallback); generated compact tables enable this
option automatically.

