\subsection{Thumbindex}
In your document you can use the following commands for displaying or switching
off a thumbindex \emph{locally}.
\begin{docCommand}{printThumbIndex}{\oarg{color}\marg{string}}
  Set the tab's \meta{color} and displayed \meta{string} for following pages.
  Place this command before a \cs{clearpage} if the new tab should appear on
  the next page.
\end{docCommand}

\begin{docCommand}{printThumbIndexOff}{}
  Remove the tab from following pages.  This changes visibility only.
\end{docCommand}

\begin{docCommand}{resetThumbIndex}{}
  Reset the thumb-index slot counter.  The next \cs{printThumbIndex} call
  starts at the first slot again.  This changes only the position counter;
  use \cs{printThumbIndexOff} separately if the current tab should be hidden.
\end{docCommand}


\subsection{ORCiD}
The ORCiD is foremost used in the CV section and stated in the entry of a
person. So there is no need for a manual insertion of it. However you can do so
using one of two following commands.

\begin{docCommand}{ORCiD}{\marg{number}}
  Display the ORCiD with the given \meta{number}.
  Adding the ORCiD logo in front.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-an-orcid-from-metadata}.
\end{docCommand}

\begin{docCommand}{getORCiD}{\marg{id}}
  Display the ORCiD of the person with the given \meta{id}
  If there is no |orcid|-entry in the database for this person, then a warning will be
  thrown and you can read about in the |.log|-file.

  See \dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-an-orcid-from-metadata}.
\end{docCommand}


\begin{dfgExample}[label={ex:manual-an-orcid-from-metadata}]{An ORCiD from metadata}
\ORCiD{0000-0000-0000-0001}\par
\getORCiD{per1}\par
\getORCiDvalue{per1}
\end{dfgExample}

\section{Bibliography/Publication}
\subsection{Equal contribution}
In some cases it is very important to state that two or more authors
have contributed equally to a publication.
Take the following publication as example:
\begin{dispListing}
  Schneider, K.M.*, C. Elfers*, A. Ghallab, C.V. Schneider,
  E.J.C. Galvez, A. Mohs, W. Gui, L.S. Candels, T.H. Wirtz,
  S. Zuehlke, M. Spiteller, M. Myllys, A. Roulet, A.
  Ouzerdine, B. Lelouvier, K. Kilic, L. Liao, A. Nier, E.
  Latz, I. Bergheim, C.A. Thaiss, J.G. Hengstler, T.
  Strowig, and C. Trautwein. 2020. Intestinal Dysbiosis
  Amplifies Acetaminophen-Induced Acute Liver Injury. Cell.
  Mol. Gastroenterol. Hepatol. 11(4):909-933. *equal contribution
\end{dispListing}
The first authors have \texttt{equal contribution}.
In the bibtex-entry add \emph{two} lines:
\begin{dispListing}
  author+an       = {1=equal;2=equal},
  addendum        = equal,
\end{dispListing}
With \texttt{author+an} you annotate the authors, the numbers refer to the
position of the author in the authors's list.

The complete entry of the mentioned example looks like:

\begin{dispListing}
@article{schneider2020a,
  author          = {Schneider, Kai Markus and Elfers, Carsten and Ghallab,
      Ahmed and Schneider, Carolin Victoria and Galvez, Eric J.C.
      and Mohs, Antje and Gui, Wenfang and Candels, Lena Susanna and
      Wirtz, Theresa Hildegard and Zuehlke, Sebastian and Spiteller,
      Michael and Myllys, Maiju and Roulet, Alain and Ouzerdine,
      Amirouche and Lelouvier, Benjamin and Kilic, Konrad and Liao,
      Lijun and Nier, Anika and Latz, Eicke and Bergheim, Ina and
      Thaiss, Christoph A. and Hengstler, Jan G. and Strowig, Till
      and Trautwein, Christian},
  author+an       = {1=equal;2=equal},
  date            = 2020,
  title           = {Intestinal Dysbiosis Amplifies Acetaminophen-Induced Acute
      Liver Injury},
  volume          = 11,
  pages           = {909--933},
  language        = {pt},
  journal         = {Cellular and Molecular Gastroenterology and Hepatology},
  doi             = {10.1016/j.jcmgh.2020.11.002},
  number          = 4,
  source          = {Crossref},
  url             = {https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jcmgh.2020.11.002},
  publisher       = {Elsevier BV},
  issn            = {2352-345X},
  date            = 2021,
  addendum        = equal,
}
\end{dispListing}
\subsection{Shared last authorship}
Very similar to the \texttt{equal contribution}-part there is also a way to
specify authors who \texttt{shared last authorship}.

In the bibtex-entry add \emph{two} lines:

\begin{dispListing}
  author+an       = {1=sharedLast;2=sharedLast},
  addendum        = sharedLast,
\end{dispListing}

Here is a complete example.

\begin{dispListing}
@Article{roessner2021a,
  Author          = {Roessner, P.M. and Cid, L.Llao and Lupar, E. and Roider,
      T. and Bordas, M. and Schifflers, C. and Arseni, L. and
      Gaupel, A.C. and Kilpert, F. and Krotschel, M. and
      Arnold, S.J. and Sellner, L. and Colomer, D. and
      Stilgenbauer, S. and Dietrich, S. and Lichter, P. and
      Izcue, A. and Seiffert, M.},
  Date            = {2021},
  author+an       = {17=sharedLast;18=sharedLast},
  Addendum        = sharedLast,
  Keywords        = {MM-A},
  Title           = {{EOMES} and {IL}-10 regulate antitumor activity of T
      regulatory type 1 {CD4(+)} T cells in chronic lymphocytic
      leukemia},
  Volume          = {35},
  Pages           = {2311--2324},
  Language        = {en},
  JournalTitle    = {Leukemia}
}
\end{dispListing}


\subsection{Emphasizing authors}
\label{sec:doc:bold-authors}
You can have certain authors in bold letters in your bibliography section to
highlight e.g. the core people of the proposal.

\textbf{Notice:} This feature is automatically triggered (unless switched off, see \refKey{emphasizeAuthors})
for the CV section (see \ref{sec:doc:cv-basic}). We explain its function for additional use.


To add a person to the (sectioned) bibliography use \cs{addboldnames}.

\begin{docCommand}{addboldnames}{
  \marg{\brackets{nn},
    \brackets{nn},
    \brackets{nn}}
}

Displaying authors in an emphasized way.
\end{docCommand}

Notice that for each variation of a person’s name you have to add one set of
\marg{nn}.
For example you have an author with different spellings in your |.bib|-file, you
have to list all of them to get them in bold letters (or adjust the entries in
your |.bib|-file).

\begin{dispListing}
  \addboldnames{
    {Max Doe},
    {M. Doe},
    {Doe, Max H.},
    {Doe, Max Heinrich}
  }
\end{dispListing}

After a (sectioned) bibliography and you do not want to display the before
defined authors in bold letters anymore, you can use \cs{resetboldnames}:
\begin{docCommand}{resetboldnames}{}
  Switch off showing the under \refCom{addboldnames} defined names.
\end{docCommand}


In case you do not need to have authors in bold letters
you can disable the feature modification done in \dfgFile{dfg-customization}.


\subsection{Providing publication information}
\label{sec:manual-cv-publications}

Providing the most relevant publications is done through the referenced
|.bib|-file.
Within a bibtex-entry you need to add a keyword with certain values to it
preceeded with the ID of the person.

\begin{itemize}
  \item[-A:]Category A – Articles in peer-reviewed journals, contributions to
        peer-reviewed conferences or to anthology volumes, and book publications
  \item[-B:] Category B – Any other form of published results
\end{itemize}
For example:
\begin{dispListing}
  @article{...,
      keywords = {<ID>-A},
    }
\end{dispListing}

To bold the complete bibliography entry only in Project B06, add its
project-specific highlight keyword alongside any publication keywords:
\begin{dispListing}
  @article{...,
    keywords = {pabst-A,B06-highlight},
  }
\end{dispListing}
This affects project bibliography lists, including project-report references
and published-result lists.  The same entry is not bolded in other projects
unless their own \texttt{<project-id>-highlight} keyword is present.  Set
\texttt{emphasizeEntries=true} in \cs{changeSettings} to enable this feature.
Only one highlight keyword per distinct project is needed, even when several
PIs from that project coauthor the publication.  For a publication to be bold
in both B06 and Q04, use each keyword once:
\begin{dispListing}
  @article{...,
    keywords = {pabst-A,B06-highlight,Q04-highlight},
  }
\end{dispListing}
The optional \texttt{B06-project} keyword can record project membership for
other workflows, but it does not activate \refKey{emphasizeEntries}.  Existing
CV-selection keywords such as \texttt{pabst-A} can stay in the same field.

For the frame-text bibliography in chapter 1, add
\texttt{frametext-highlight} and use \refCom{printFrameBibliography}:
\begin{dispListing}
  @article{...,
    keywords = {pabst-A,B06-highlight,frametext-highlight},
  }
\end{dispListing}
With \texttt{emphasizeEntries=true}, this entry is bold in both the B06
project bibliography and the frame-text bibliography. Neither keyword
changes formatting in other bibliography contexts or in inline citations.

Of course several people can use the same reference for this section:

\begin{dispListing}
  @article{...,
      keywords = {<ID1>-A, <ID2>-B, <ID3>-A},
    }
\end{dispListing}

Open-access markers are handled by |biblatex-ext-oa|. If the API-based
open-access request does not work for a publication, add the fallback
annotation directly to the relevant |.bib| entry:

\begin{dispListing}
  @article{...,
      options         = {openaccess=auto},
      doi+an:default  = {=openaccess},
    }
\end{dispListing}



\section{Automatic wording and list cardinality}
\paragraph{Automatic singular, plural, and cardinality-dependent output}

The class derives the following visible wording and layout from the number of
metadata items.  These are the places where changing a one-item list to two or
more items changes more than the retrieved data itself.

\begin{description}
  \item[Project PIs]
    \refCom{printProjectHead} counts the comma-separated PI ids in the
    project's \refKey{ref-person-id} field.  Exactly one id selects the
    singular branch; two or more ids select the plural branch.  The field is
    required, so an empty list is invalid metadata; internally it falls back
    to the singular branch.

    This PI count controls all of the following output:
    \par\noindent\textbullet\space the heading \enquote{Project leader} or
        \enquote{Project leaders};
    \par\noindent\textbullet\space the \cs{getPersonInformation} layout, which
        shows one PI in a single card and several PIs in a two-column raster
        of cards;
    \par\noindent\textbullet\space contract question C1, which uses
        \enquote{project leader at the institution} or
        \enquote{project leaders at the institutions};
    \par\noindent\textbullet\space contract question C2, which switches between
        \enquote{Does the above mentioned person hold a fixed-term position?}
        and \enquote{Do any of the above mentioned persons hold fixed-term
        positions?};
    \par\noindent\textbullet\space contract question C3, which switches between
        \enquote{Will the above mentioned person} and
        \enquote{Will one or more of the above-mentioned persons};
    \par\noindent\textbullet\space contract question C4, which again uses singular or plural
        \enquote{project leader(s)} and \enquote{institution(s)}; and
    \par\noindent\textbullet\space contract answers, where PI surnames are appended in parentheses
        only when several PIs must be distinguished.  Lists of dates or other
        follow-up values likewise append surnames when more than one matching
        PI contributes a value.

  \item[Research areas]
    In \refCom{printProjectHead}, one value in the project's
    \refKey{research-area} field produces the heading
    \enquote{Research area}; two or more values produce
    \enquote{Research areas}.

  \item[Applicant affiliations]
    \refCom{dfgApplicantAffiliationsTF} selects its first argument for one id
    in |meta.ref-affiliation-id| and its second argument for two or more ids.
    This helper controls:
    \par\noindent\textbullet\space whether the title page prints an additional-applicant block;
    \par\noindent\textbullet\space \enquote{applicant university} or
        \enquote{applicant universities} in the existing-direct-costs table;
    \par\noindent\textbullet\space the Chapter~1 heading \enquote{Research profile of the applicant
        university/universities};
    \par\noindent\textbullet\space \enquote{coordinating university/universities} in the Chapter~4
        bylaws heading; and
    \par\noindent\textbullet\space both the noun and verb in the Chapter~5 declaration:
        \enquote{The applicant university confirms} or
        \enquote{The applicant universities confirm}.

    On the title page, \cs{dfgOtherApplicantAffiliationsTF} applies the
    same rule after the first, coordinating affiliation has been removed:
    exactly two applicant affiliations in total produce
    \enquote{Other applicant university}, while three or more produce
    \enquote{Other applicant universities}.

  \item[Existing-staff overview]
    \refCom{OverviewExistingStaffTable} uses the number of applicant
    affiliations for its spanning group heading: one applicant id produces
    \enquote{applicant university}, while several produce
    \enquote{applicant universities}.  Other affiliation columns do not
    affect this wording.

  \item[One-, two-, and longer-item lists]
    Several formatters change punctuation rather than nouns.  In prose,
    \refCom{getPI}, \refCom{getProject}, \refCom{getProjectPI}, and
    \refCom{getPIProject} print one item without a separator, two items with
    \enquote{and}, and three or more with commas plus a final
    \enquote{and}.  Project and PI references inside tables instead use compact
    slash separators.  The complete applicant-name, applicant-location, and
    other-applicant lists exposed by \cs{theCRCaffiliation},
    \cs{theCRCaffiliationLocation}, and
    \cs{theCRCOtherApplicantAffiliations} use the same natural-language rule.
    \refCom{printList}|{ResearchAreasFromProjects}| uses a comma for exactly
    two items and a final \enquote{and} for lists of three or more.
\end{description}

Fixed text written directly in project content is not altered automatically;
authors must choose its singular or plural form themselves.

\section{Project-area entries in the contents}
\begin{docCommand}{dfgTOCprojectPart}{\marg{letter}\oarg{label}\marg{title}}
  Add a project-area heading to the table of contents.  The \meta{letter}
  identifies the project area, \meta{label} defaults to \texttt{Project area},
  and \meta{title} is the text printed after the area label.
\end{docCommand}

\begin{dispListing*}{title={Wrap a project section.}}
  \dfgTOCprojectPart{A}{Microbiota and metabolic pathways}
  \printProjectHead{A01}
  \ldots
  \printProjectTail
\end{dispListing*}


\section{Optional inputs and diagnostics}
\begin{docCommand}{checkFile}{\marg{file}}
  Input \meta{file} if it exists.  If the file is missing, compilation
  continues and a warning is written to the log.  This is used for optional
  metadata, customization, and content files.
\end{docCommand}


\section{Troubleshooting}

The class reports missing or inconsistent data in the terminal and the
\texttt{.log} file.  If your editor does not collect those messages, this
command shows the class messages together:

\begin{dispListing*}{title={Show DFG class messages in a log file}}
grep 'Package dfg' <filename>.log
\end{dispListing*}

\begin{description}
  \item[\texttt{Unknown database name}] The database has not been loaded, or
    the database name was misspelled.  Check the relevant metadata file and
    whether it is input by the class.
  \item[Missing or empty key warnings] The row exists, but the requested field
    is empty.  This is intentional where the template needs to alert the user,
    for example for missing ORCiD values.
  \item[\texttt{Not allowed in LR mode}] Vertical material such as
    \cs{vspace}, \cs{addvspace}, or a paragraph break was inserted in a table
    place that only accepts horizontal material.  Prefer the documented table
    and list commands, or use options such as \texttt{narrow} for compact table
    output.
  \item[Stale output] Regenerate the class with \texttt{make files}, and check
    whether the document uses the generated class from \texttt{local/}.
\end{description}

