\section{Release workflow for maintainers}

For a new release, update \cs{dfgVersion} and \cs{dfgVersionDate} at the top of
\texttt{crc-proposal-documentation-and-code.dtx}, the release line in the
README block, the changelog, and \texttt{dfg-crc.pkg}.  Keep these values in
one release commit.

\begin{dispListing*}{title={Release and CTAN checks}}
make ctan-check
make check
% Optional online validation without submission:
make ctan-validate
\end{dispListing*}

The focused targets \texttt{make test-command-options} and
\texttt{make test-participating-institutions} run 30 argument/option checks and
10 layout cases, respectively. The command-options fixture also checks
14 document-text cases: the generic date, early customization, later settings,
direct redefinitions, deferred references, and local scope. Both targets are
included in \texttt{make check}.
The general regression fixture lives in \texttt{ci/test-template.tex}; its
build copy is \texttt{local/test-template.tex}. The manual scratch file
\texttt{local/test.tex} is neither generated nor compiled by these checks.

The first command builds the manual and the curated
\texttt{output/ctan/dfg-crc.zip}, verifies its exact allow-list, extracts it in
a clean temporary directory, runs the portable installer, and compiles a
minimal document against the resulting class.  The online target validates but
does not submit the archive.  Before committing, inspect
\texttt{git status --short}, run \texttt{git diff --check}, and review the ZIP
file list.

\subsection{Maintaining executable examples}
The driver uses |documentation=true| to keep the class typography and rendering
commands while loading its fixtures explicitly. This option defaults to false;
it suppresses automatic proposal customization, metadata, and bibliography
inputs. It is intended for this manual, not for ordinary proposals.
The fixtures and the manual driver are maintained in the DTX. A documentation
build extracts them again, so the manual needs no private proposal files.

Add runnable examples with the manual's |dfgExample| environment. Its required
argument is the title; add a stable |label={ex:manual-name}| option and refer
to it from the relevant command description with |\dfgExampleRef{ex:manual-name}|.
Add |dfg wide| to the options for full-width output
below the code. Each example executes the displayed code in a local group.
Floating output is set to non-floating inside the result panel. Use the
fictional IDs |per1|, |per2|, |ins1|, |aff1|, and |A01| where possible.
Use |dfg project=A03| on the example environment when an example needs a
current-project context, and explain that context in the surrounding text.
Keep data-only declarations, incomplete preamble fragments, full-document
project wrappers, external commands, and BibTeX records in |dispListing|.
The shared project and PI metadata are listed once with |\dfgTableSetup|
in the reference chapter; live table examples and the generated local proposal
use those same records. Maintain matching |cv-person-id| career records in the
|meta-cv| block and fictional bibliography records with |person-id-A| or
|person-id-B| keywords for every PI included in the example CV appendix.
Project-boundary examples show labelled excerpts from the standalone proposal,
because those commands change pages, headers, and bibliography scope.
Running |make doc| checks that the PDFs contain all fifteen active project
chapters, the ending-project example, and twelve PI CVs before compiling the
manual. Inspect the PDF
as well as the build log after changing a layout example.

\chapter{Complete example proposal}
\label{chap:complete-example-proposal}
The following pages show the complete fictional proposal generated from this
DTX using the same metadata as the documentation examples: twelve PIs, six
projects in area A, six in area B, and three in area Q. The title page, general
information, financial overviews, all fifteen active project descriptions, the
former project A00, bylaws, declarations, and back matter are included.
The scientific text, people, figures, and funding values are illustrative.

The proposal is built with |final=true|, which suppresses draft tags and margin
diagnostics. Each imported page is reduced to 82\% and framed, with an
\enquote{Example proposal} label above it. The proposal's own page numbers,
headers, and thumb index remain inside the frame; the manual page number appears
below it, clearly separating the two documents.
For the proposal's own clickable references and bookmarks, open the accompanying
\href{dfg-crc-proposal-example.pdf}{standalone example proposal PDF}.

To regenerate the manual and both complete examples from the DTX, run |make doc| in the template
repository. Use |make proposal-doc-example| to build only the standalone
example. Both the local proposal and the final-mode documentation build use
the same metadata and chapter inputs in |local/|. Its generated wrapper is:
