\section{Research profile of the \crc} %1.2
\begin{dfgSummaryBox}[title={Spotlights: Moving Forward}]
  \item Resolve causal mechanisms of communication between microbial
    communities, epithelial barriers, and tissue-resident immune cells.
  \item Combine spatial profiling, experimental perturbation, and predictive
    modelling across the participating projects.
  \item Translate shared methods into reproducible services, interoperable
    data, and structured training for researchers in early career phases.
\end{dfgSummaryBox}

%% \FloatBarrier % no images in references list.
\defbibheading{frame-references}[References]{\subsection*{#1}}
\printFrameBibliography[heading=frame-references]


\subsection{Summary of the research programme}%1.2.1

The CRC investigates how microbial and host-derived signals cross epithelial
barriers and influence organ function.  During the previous funding periods,
the consortium established shared cohorts, experimental models, imaging
workflows, and computational methods.  The new programme links these resources
in hypothesis-driven projects that test causality across molecular, cellular,
and tissue scales.

\subsection{Detailed presentation of the research programme}%1.2.2

Project area A examines microbial metabolites and barrier signalling, whereas
project area B focuses on immune circuits and tissue responses.  The central
projects provide imaging, analysis, training, coordination, and research-data
services.  Common samples, harmonised metadata, and reciprocal collaborations
connect the individual work programmes and enable validation across models and
participating institutions.

\subsection{Positioning of the \crc within its general research area}%1.2.3

The programme combines mechanistic microbiome research with systems immunology
and organ physiology.  Its distinguishing feature is the coordinated
validation of candidate signals from discovery cohorts through controlled
experimental systems to tissue-level readouts, supported by shared technology
and transparent data-management standards.

\subsection{National and international cooperation and networking} %1.2.4

The CRC maintains method-oriented collaborations with national infrastructure
networks and international partner laboratories.  Cooperation agreements
define sample and data transfer, authorship, quality assurance, and access to
specialised technologies.  Visiting-researcher and exchange formats are
coordinated through the Integrated Research Training Group.

\section{Research profile of the applicant
  \dfgApplicantAffiliationsTF{university}{universities}} %1.3
\subsection{Strategy and planning}%1.3.1

Example University has identified host--microbiome research as a strategic
priority and supports the CRC through permanent scientific positions, shared
technical platforms, research-data infrastructure, and central administration.
Annual reviews align institutional investments with the scientific milestones
of the consortium.

\subsection{Staff situation} % 1.3.2

The participating units contribute principal investigators, research staff,
technical personnel, data stewards, and administrative support.  Recruitment
planning prioritises timely appointments, transparent selection procedures,
career development, and continuity of essential technical expertise.

\subsection{Research infrastructure} % 1.3.3

The CRC has access to advanced microscopy, flow cytometry, sequencing,
gnotobiotic facilities, secure data storage, and high-performance computing.
Central projects coordinate access, training, maintenance, and quality control
so that methods and datasets remain comparable across all projects.

\section{Support structures} % 1.4
\subsection{Researchers in early career phases} % 1.4.1

Researchers in early career phases receive structured scientific and career
mentoring, transferable-skills training, annual progress reviews, and access to
methods courses and exchange opportunities organised by \getProject{Q02}.

