\subsection{Knowledge transfer and science communication} % 1.4.3

The CRC communicates results through open research resources, public lectures,
school and patient-group activities, and dialogue formats with clinical and
societal stakeholders.  Transfer opportunities are assessed early, with clear
procedures for intellectual property, responsible communication, and reusable
research outputs.

\section{Risks in international cooperation} %1.5

International partnerships are reviewed for legal, ethical, export-control,
data-protection, and research-security risks.  Written agreements specify
permitted data and material transfers, access rights, publication principles,
and responsibilities.  The participating universities reassess collaborations
when partners, technologies, or regulatory conditions change.

\section{Considerations on aspects of ecological sustainability in project
  planning and implementation} %1.6

The CRC considers ecological sustainability when planning experiments,
procurement, travel, computing, sample storage, and shared infrastructure.
Projects consolidate measurement campaigns, share equipment and reference
materials, select proportionate data-retention and computing strategies, and
prefer lower-emission travel or remote participation where this is compatible
with the scientific objective.  The steering committee reviews these measures
alongside scientific feasibility, research quality, legal obligations, animal
welfare, and the responsible use of resources.


